window.harnessChunks["rows-8f87ec4bc66f708d.js"]=["<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T18:14:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpeOginni/gitterm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitTerm</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpeOginni/gitterm/tree/1465ca1b3683a24966ce0815129742460b16c13e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1465ca1b3683a24966ce0815129742460b16c13e\">1465ca1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute repository coding tasks via a model-driven OpenCode agent inside a supported provisioned remote workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown codebase states, task requirements, implementation errors, model/provider variability and missing context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose coding edits, shell/tool interactions and task responses to a submitted prompt or next plan item. - Decision owner: configured external OpenCode model agent running in a first-party GitTerm-created and addressed session; its internals are not transferred as GitTerm management rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: createAgentRun, runtime.createSession, runtime.prompt, SDK run events, terminal/browser workspace access, provider adapter and input callback relay. - Closure path: user-specified repository/prompt → live model coding session → tool/messaging events and results → completed/failed/awaiting-input response or next plan iteration. - Boundary reachability: account-authenticated run.create invokes createAgentRun, creates a native session and calls prompt; the SDK exposes the same route, while optional Cloudflare loop starts an agent run with a plan-file task instruction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: actual choice of implementation commands and edits is not specified by the GitTerm queue; first-party orchestration explicitly composes a real external coding agent for the selected task. - Evidence: pinned agent-run/index.ts, OpenCode adapter, SDK README and Cloudflare loop prompt. - Basis: reachable hosted/sandboxed model-agent execution surface. - Confidence: high on dispatch and action ownership, not actual correctness. - Caveats: no live remote workspace, compute provider or model session was executed for this source review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party adaptive mediation of ongoing peer S1 work-unit interference is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple users, shared workspace access, concurrent SDK watchers and cloud provider instances can contend, but implementation treats these as authorization, transport or run serialization issues. - Decisive decision or feedback right: API limits continuation of a native session while previous run is active; Redis watcher leases avoid duplicate lifecycle observers; user-initiated sharing enables teammates to access a workspace. - Decision owner: constructor-written concurrency checks, scoped tokens and transport lease service, not a peer-unit conflict negotiator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: active-run status guard, idempotency key, cluster watcher lease, workspace share permission and account routing. - Closure path: a run is rejected/serialized or a watcher elected; no inter-agent operational conflict → adaptive coordination decision → changed future peer relations is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple client connections or compute instances are not independent VSM S1 units whose contradictions GitTerm resolves; neither is an operator&#39;s manual share link. - Evidence: pinned agent-run/index.ts, agent-run/cluster.ts, SDK README and product README. - Basis: scoped negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an external collaborative coding team may coordinate its members outside GitTerm.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: preserve current workspace fleet viability and control active compute/run commitments against idle time, quota, retention and caller lifetime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: zombie or unbounded runtimes, exhausted user plans, stale idle sessions, abandoned paused resources, multiple simultaneous loop runs and failed runtime startup. - Decisive decision or feedback right: pause idle/quota-exhausted workspaces, terminate beyond retention/lifetime, enforce no active same-loop run and max loop count, cancel/settle abandoned runs. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic API and worker control logic (idle-reaper, metering, workspace service and loop gates), under operator/admin-selected settings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: account/workspace DB state, Redis activity signal, usage sessions, configured plan thresholds, getIdleWorkspaces/getQuotaExceededWorkspaces/getLongTermInactiveWorkspaces, internal pause/terminate endpoints and run state machine. - Closure path: current fleet activity/usage/plan state → rule-based classification of excess/idle/expired resources → API pause/terminate and usage bookkeeping → next fleet poll or explicit resume/cleanup. - Boundary reachability: scheduled idle-reaper runs query internal endpoints and issue actual pauseWorkspaceInternal/terminateWorkspaceInternal mutations; the plan-loop router enforces max-runs/active-run gates and quota before dispatch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: resource acceptance, pause, resume and termination are specified by constructor thresholds and user policy; the external coding model has no autonomous control-plane authority over other workspaces. - Whole-system current view: active/paused/expired managed workspaces and their user quotas and activity across the deployment, plus run-count/current status per plan loop. - Current-control decision scope: sandbox resource and execution lifecycle commitments, not business-level goal prioritization or final code acceptance. - Evidence: pinned reaper, metering, agent-loop router and SDK lifecycle. - Basis: closed first-party cross-workspace current-resource regulation path. - Confidence: medium-high for managed mode, narrower self-hosted case. - Caveats: idle and quota features may be off in self-hosted deployments; explicit caller lifetime and run gates are still separate constructor paths, and quota-driven pause is not an evaluation of code quality.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent substantive inspection challenges the coding agent&#39;s implementation claims or the current resource manager&#39;s view of output quality. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code regressions, missed user requirements and false agent success claims remain outside the platform&#39;s own independent audit function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: SDK returns native model messages and status; no separately governed read-only code reviewer or correction authority is established by these transports. - Decision owner: OpenCode runtime and its status watcher supply routine reports, while human collaborators may inspect results externally. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session snapshot, message/tool normalization, run lifecycle events, idempotency and event callbacks. - Closure path: model result → normalized status/stream → human or caller; no distinct artifact sampling/critical findings → S3 corrective response → re-audit loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: watcher reconciliation is operational bookkeeping, and a prompt like “review PR” still uses the same ordinary run without a first-party independent challenge mandate. - Evidence: pinned agent-runtime/opencode.ts, agent-run/watcher.ts, packages/sdk/src/runs.ts and README. - Basis: bounded independence negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: caller can build an external reviewing integration with the SDK, but it is not shipped as mandatory or a distinct first-party ownership path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no environmental/future option-building loop to adapt GitTerm&#39;s later coding capability or platform strategy is present. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing provider capabilities, repositories and model ecosystems are handled through configured integrations and current user actions rather than first-party strategic sensing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user chooses providers/models/plan file and may run another feature iteration; no agent is commissioned to generate future capability options from an external horizon and feed adoption to S3. - Decision owner: external operator and fixed first-party plan/loop rules; coding agent chooses the next current feature, not the platform&#39;s future governance/capability portfolio. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider registry, model settings, plan/progress file, bounded maxRuns, callback completion and git commit path. - Closure path: current plan file → one implementation run → updated progress/commit → possible next run; no scanned future environment → prospective alternative design → S3 option selection → changed future system capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: iterative implementation of a static user-provided product plan is continuing S1 delivery, not outside-and-then organizational intelligence. - Evidence: pinned plan-loop router, agent-loop service, Cloudflare generatePrompt, product README. - Basis: scoped negative despite real agentic iterations. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: caller may program future research via custom SDK prompts, but an arbitrary caller-authored loop is not inherited by this repository.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: ultimate mission, authorization, risk appetite and repository legitimacy remain with user/admin/provider governance outside GitTerm&#39;s agent runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: credential scope, permission approvals, workspace access, private repository rights and acceptable cloud spending. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user/admin configures providers, quota plans and access; SDK handler/human answers an agent&#39;s permission request; first-party code enforces the returned choice rather than deciding constitutional purpose. - Decision owner: external account owner, admin, credential provider and (for individual questions) the SDK&#39;s human callback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: authenticated account routes, scoped workspace token, requested permission reply, plan settings, quota boundaries and workspace lifecycle. - Closure path: user-selected purpose/authorization → agent run → per-tool permission or question → handler response → resumed/blocked run; no identity-level dilemma → legitimate parent adjudication → mission-policy decision → binding return to subsequent system operation is furnished as a first-party S5 loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user granting a shell command or provisioning a cloud credential exercises operational permission, not a GitTerm-owned right to decide ultimate organizational identity. - Evidence: pinned SDK README, runs.ts, API run route, product README and credential-security docs. - Basis: bounded negative at declared workspace-service recursion. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: parent organization and user may have an external S5; this review does not inherit it.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e4069e8f057f44f1ae8b14a1d869334727037b08/assessments/gi/gitterm.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e4069e8f057f44f1ae8b14a1d869334727037b08\">e4069e8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:15:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenClaw</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-16\">2026-09-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/tree/387da5edaa9d6c6cfcfc15e167fe2e46d8647d86\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 387da5edaa9d6c6cfcfc15e167fe2e46d8647d86\">387da5e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: a standard configured assistant autonomously uses model/harness tools toward user work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: authorized sibling/parent/child messaging and session ownership/lifecycle tools provide a concrete constructor path for operational agents to exchange coordination information and regulate responsibility, but no standard agent-owned anti-oscillation policy is closed out of the box.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the existing agent-visible status/history, lifecycle, ownership, spawn/cancel and model-selection surfaces remain a material S3 constructor path without a stock autonomous whole-system regulator. In addition, the first-party system-agent closes a distinct parent-governed S3 mode: it exposes current whole-deployment state, proposes concrete model/config/team/gateway/plugin control changes, binds the decisive mutation to exact operator approval, and returns the approved change through canonical writers/control operations into subsequent runtime behavior. The parent mode is therefore independently complete while autonomous S3 ownership still requires composition.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: audit facts, telemetry, security controls and transcript inspection are observability/enforcement surfaces; no distinct sufficiently independent autonomous complementary-audit path is supplied in the reviewed standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: heartbeats, cron, external triggers, dreaming and system-agent repair/setup concern current operation or maintenance; they do not establish a distinct external-and-prospective adaptation function whose options feed back into present capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: operator approval of configuration/setup mutations is not by itself identity or ultimate-policy closure. Security, visibility, pairing and tool policy remain operator/runtime configured, but the reviewed evidence does not reconstruct an S5-specific identity/policy issue reaching legitimate ultimate authority and returning as an authoritative identity/policy decision. The S3 parent mode must not be promoted to S5 merely because the operator has final say over a current-control mutation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0b75c7fb0539831a89e5ca33f4cf1df430f3550f/assessments/openclaw.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0b75c7fb0539831a89e5ca33f4cf1df430f3550f\">0b75c7f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T22:07:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Akash-nath29/Coderrr\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Coderrr</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Akash-nath29/Coderrr/tree/933dd35a6f35fbb54f3e8a80a75356af9c0ff7f1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 933dd35a6f35fbb54f3e8a80a75356af9c0ff7f1\">933dd35 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform an approved specification into code and verified task outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain project structure, tool errors, failed acceptance checks and unexpected code behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose inspections, edits, sandbox commands, corrective next actions and task completion. - Decision owner: coding model agent after the ordinary plan authorization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session phase transition, ToolRegistry, local filesystem, sandbox, SpecStore, budget and HTTP provider transport. - Closure path: registry executes model calls; observations become ToolResultBlocks in the next model request; the agent revises work and chooses finish/updatetask. - Boundary reachability: Session.execute directly invokes runtask/runconversation using the installed registry; no developer-authored custom agent composition is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves gates and persistence but no equivalent code-change choices. Human authorization limits the task without supplying those choices. - Evidence: src/coderrr/agent/loop.py, src/coderrr/agent/session.py, src/coderrr/prompts/system.py, src/coderrr/tools/system/review.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: static supported-path evidence, not measured task success; MCP tools may have external side effects even during planning once allowed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Interference regulation among distinct operational units; not established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying function-specific regulation established beyond ordinary coding-task variety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The reviewed runtime has one sequential operational actor; an inner tool loop and outer retry loop do not form distinct interacting S1 units. Batched calls are executed sequentially. - Decision owner: no first-party owner for the absent organizational function; ordinary task/configuration rights stay with the actors described above. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: relevant mechanisms are recorded here without inheriting ownership. - Closure path: no qualifying function-specific closure found in the checked modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent performing ordinary code work or a stateless verifier call does not by itself own this separate organizational function. - Evidence: src/coderrr/agent/loop.py, src/coderrr/tools/registry.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high; bounded to inspected distribution. - Caveats: not a claim of universal impossibility or external deployment absence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Whole-system current operational cohesion and intervention; not established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying function-specific regulation established beyond ordinary coding-task variety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Session presents the plan and task list, but the human merely authorizes one coding operation. Tool budgets and write policies enforce configured limits; the model&#39;s per-task planning is ordinary operation. No broader shared-resource/commitment regulator or specific S3 constructor is supplied. - Decision owner: no first-party owner for the absent organizational function; ordinary task/configuration rights stay with the actors described above. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: relevant mechanisms are recorded here without inheriting ownership. - Closure path: no qualifying function-specific closure found in the checked modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent performing ordinary code work or a stateless verifier call does not by itself own this separate organizational function. - Evidence: src/coderrr/agent/session.py, src/coderrr/config.py, src/coderrr/tools/system/review.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high; bounded to inspected distribution. - Caveats: not a claim of universal impossibility or external deployment absence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independent complementary challenge to ordinary operational reports; not established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying function-specific regulation established beyond ordinary coding-task variety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Verifier receives a proposed write and current content in a fresh model request and can reject it. This is a mandatory ordinary production check per write, not alternative access challenging the producer&#39;s completion/reporting claims. Its independent call context alone does not establish S3. Sandbox tests are also normal self-verification. - Decision owner: no first-party owner for the absent organizational function; ordinary task/configuration rights stay with the actors described above. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: relevant mechanisms are recorded here without inheriting ownership. - Closure path: no qualifying function-specific closure found in the checked modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent performing ordinary code work or a stateless verifier call does not by itself own this separate organizational function. - Evidence: src/coderrr/verify.py, src/coderrr/tools/write/files.py, src/coderrr/prompts/system.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high; bounded to inspected distribution. - Caveats: not a claim of universal impossibility or external deployment absence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: External prospective intelligence returned as capability adaptation; not established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying function-specific regulation established beyond ordinary coding-task variety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Skills search fetches task guidance and MCP adds configured tools; planning develops the current requested solution. Neither traces external/future distinctions into prospective adaptation options that change the harness&#39;s present capability through a corresponding current-control loop. - Decision owner: no first-party owner for the absent organizational function; ordinary task/configuration rights stay with the actors described above. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: relevant mechanisms are recorded here without inheriting ownership. - Closure path: no qualifying function-specific closure found in the checked modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent performing ordinary code work or a stateless verifier call does not by itself own this separate organizational function. - Evidence: src/coderrr/skills/loader.py, src/coderrr/prompts/system.py, src/coderrr/config.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high; bounded to inspected distribution. - Caveats: not a claim of universal impossibility or external deployment absence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimate identity/ultimate-policy closure; not established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying function-specific regulation established beyond ordinary coding-task variety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Static Coderrr identity/safety instructions, mode restrictions and tool consent constrain work. askreview handles requirements and blockers; no function-specific identity/policy dispute reaches a legitimate ultimate authority and returns an authoritative policy that governs later operation. - Decision owner: no first-party owner for the absent organizational function; ordinary task/configuration rights stay with the actors described above. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: relevant mechanisms are recorded here without inheriting ownership. - Closure path: no qualifying function-specific closure found in the checked modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent performing ordinary code work or a stateless verifier call does not by itself own this separate organizational function. - Evidence: src/coderrr/prompts/system.py, src/coderrr/tools/system/review.py, src/coderrr/agent/session.py, src/coderrr/tools/registry.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high; bounded to inspected distribution. - Caveats: not a claim of universal impossibility or external deployment absence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a78d682d502342789826c7f3b67a40d9557ce6ba/assessments/co/coderrr.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a78d682d502342789826c7f3b67a40d9557ce6ba\">a78d682 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T19:12:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/fara\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fara</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/fara/tree/a675d6d61c41c47ae87bacefeab22caad18e3e84\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a675d6d61c41c47ae87bacefeab22caad18e3e84\">a675d6d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous first-party visual browser-task execution, with model-selected action responses to real environment feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing websites, unknown navigation, forms, state-dependent GUI controls, unexpected browser responses and missing task information. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured model decides actual computer-use function/action and arguments after observing screenshot plus task/interaction history; installed agent determines continuation, bounded exit and task-level human-response resume. - Decision owner: First-party Fara15Agent model/action feedback loop; configured model API supplies inference but not a separately authored organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PlaywrightEnvironment screenshots/page context, action schema/parser, RunContext event log, model client, max rounds, optional CAPTCHA waiting and user ask path. - Closure path: task + screenshot → model-picked browser action → first-party browser controller executes → post-screenshot/page context saved → model observes result next turn / completes task. - Boundary reachability: The packaged runfara15agent CLI constructs PlaywrightEnvironment/Fara15Agent/RunContext and directly calls agent.run; the run code executes first-party browser actions and checkpoints feedback. The same Fara instance resumes after a task-critical user reply. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concrete website action is selected online from observations by the model, not statically replayed scripted macro. Browser/tool/controller scaffolding remains deterministic and bounded. - Evidence: src/fara/runfara.py; src/fara/agents/fara/fara15agent.py; src/fara/environments/playwright/playwrightcontroller.py; src/fara/core/runcontext.py. - Basis: Implemented and CLI-wired first-party source trace, not downstream model benchmark. - Confidence: High for reachable model-guided S1 path; no hosted inference result reproduced. - Caveats: A configured endpoint is required; model may fail to comply with safety prompts, and no live web-task success is asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party inter-S1 interference/oscillation attenuation between distinct operational browser agents identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: One browser task&#39;s tool/state challenges are S1 execution uncertainty, not demonstrated interaction between two independently operating S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No distinct sibling-agent contention/resource coordination decision in shipped Fara CLI. - Decision owner: One Fara15Agent task loop and Playwright runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Single-tab browser setting, serial model actions, controller action execution and screenshot checkpoints. - Closure path: Page observation returns to the same operating agent; no cross-agent conflict → coordination response → changed sibling action feedback path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Single-tab mode, API rate limits and sequential tool actions do not by themselves supply multi-S1 coordination. - Evidence: src/fara/runfara.py; src/fara/agents/fara/fara15agent.py; src/fara/environments/playwright/environment.py. - Basis: Bounded negative first-party code path assessment. - Confidence: High for standard CLI scope. - Caveats: Another installed multi-agent orchestration framework might add S2 but that is a different system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separate, whole-system current operational controller over multiple independent ongoing browser commitments, priorities or resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task ambiguity/irreversibility and current GUI step completion are addressed by the same single-task model/operator interaction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The browser task model chooses current actions; user can confirm a critical action or provide missing details. These decisions do not regulate commitments of multiple S1 operational units. - Decision owner: Single Fara15Agent and task operator; static max-rounds/run boundaries. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WAITINGFORUSER task-status, task-time human input, controller run checkpoints and prompt-level critical-points rules. - Closure path: User prompt/approval → resume same S1 action; no separately owned whole-current regulatory decision → revised organization-wide active work commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Pausing before an irreversible browser action is current-task consent, not parent-owned S3 for a team or deployment. - Evidence: src/fara/agents/fara/fara15agent.py; src/fara/runfara.py; src/fara/agents/fara/prompts.py. - Basis: Bounded negative of current-control ownership at one browser-task recursion. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: If an external Magentic-UI instance schedules multiple Fara runs, its management is outside this repo&#39;s own CLI system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent complementary auditor with operational corrective return in first-party runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A browser action may fail or a task output may be unreliable, but ordinary screenshots/tool results return to S1 model and log. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separate first-party operational reviewer inspects raw artifacts/evidence and can challenge ordinary Fara model completion with a corrective return. - Decision owner: Same Fara15Agent for task updates; optional offline benchmark evaluator is outside the installed normal execution path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Saved trajectory events and screenshots, webeval/ benchmark judge, external Magentic-UI optional integrations. - Closure path: Trajectories are stored for later evaluation, but an auditor verdict is not fed into Fara15Agent.run to correct next actions or govern completion in the direct CLI path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Benchmark MMRubricAgent and human-labelled CUAVerifierBench can evaluate tasks but do not by mere repo proximity create a shipped operational S3 function. - Evidence: src/fara/agents/fara/fara15agent.py; src/fara/core/runcontext.py; webeval/README.md; README.md. - Basis: Negative source check separating operational runner from evaluation-only adjacent pipeline. - Confidence: Medium-high for direct Fara-1.5 CLI. - Caveats: A separately deployed Magentic-UI might add its own independent audit rights; do not transfer them.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No installed future-oriented organizational capability-adaptation options and return to present control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current webpage changes, user information and general web search task facts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent makes actions for current task; model training/paper updates and benchmark scores are upstream research, not installed agent decisions about adapting future operating capabilities. - Decision owner: External model authors/developers for training/versions and user task model for immediate browser activity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Chat history, screenshots, saved trajectories, model endpoint configuration and evaluation scripts. - Closure path: No external future capability distinctions → chosen/tested organizational adaptation → installed behavior change → subsequent operating return at assessed Fara task recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model weights having been trained or published bench results does not imply runtime S4 behavior. - Evidence: src/fara/agents/fara/fara15agent.py; README.md; webeval/README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative review of operational vs benchmark/model-development paths. - Confidence: High for installed CLI scope. - Caveats: Research teams may update model versions externally; that is not an owned task-runtime adaptation path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate identity/ethos/policy conflict settlement beyond static agent-role restrictions and user-specific task consent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Missing user data, ambiguous tasks, irreversible web submissions, action/schema identity mismatches. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User may authorize irreversible present action or supply personal detail. Static mode/identity prompt registry guards action types; no distinct ultimate-policy authority with governance return. - Decision owner: Task user and first-party model/controller according to static prompt; no runtime S5 policy function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Critical-point prompt, askuserquestion model action, WAITINGFORUSER state, identity validator, model task/input state. - Closure path: Task confirmation/input → resume same browser operation; no whole-system value/identity dispute → ultimate settlement → returned binding institution-level policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Safety-oriented user approval is welcome, but on a single task it does not confer an identity/policy governance role. - Evidence: src/fara/agents/fara/prompts.py; src/fara/agents/fara/fara15agent.py; src/fara/runfara.py. - Basis: Bounded negative source examination of critical-point and identity paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: autouserreply can automatically continue without a real person; prompt-only critical-point compliance is not an enforcement guarantee.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/868ed7dfa98f4f8c1e22d9e61990358ee75cb6bc/assessments/fara.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 868ed7dfa98f4f8c1e22d9e61990358ee75cb6bc\">868ed7d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T01:55:53+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/agent-framework-go\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft Agent Framework for Go</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/agent-framework-go/tree/f66340335e973986cbb8e9a1ae95e23596e4dc3d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f66340335e973986cbb8e9a1ae95e23596e4dc3d\">f663403 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed current task execution with first-party tool-call authorization/execution and returned environment observations in the supported tool-autocall mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unstructured requests, model-chosen tool sequences, API/tool outcome differences, errors and approval responses, stored multi-turn context and partner messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Within configured toolautocall, each provider model response can select registered tools and arguments; the first-party middleware actually executes eligible tool calls and returns result/error messages into another model request. A provider-generated final answer or stop condition terminates the request. - Decision owner: The installed model-guided Go agent operating through first-party automatic tool calling. The external model provides semantic choice; first-party Go tool registry, human approval options and middleware constrain what can execute. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ProviderConfig.Run, Agent.Run pipeline, request tool declaration/lookup, session/history and continuation, optional human approval binding, iteration/error limits and observability. - Closure path: User/input → configured Go agent/provider → model-emitted function call → tool-autocall lookup and local invocation → actual tool result or governed approval → result to provider in continuing loop → next model decision or final user-facing response. - Boundary reachability: The public Go toolautocall.New middleware can be configured into agent.Config.Middlewares and is actually called by the agent.New run pipeline, not merely a standalone example. Its run loop invokes model/provider again after dispatched tool results. Base agent.New without installed auto-call remains provider wrapper and is not independently represented as this positive loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: There is a model-selected, evidence-returning operational decision loop with registered tool effects. A bare Agent.Run provider call or deterministic sequential workflow is not the positive witness. - Evidence: agent/agent.go; agent/agent.go; agent/agent.go; agent/harness/toolautocall/autocall.go; agent/harness/toolautocall/autocall.go; agent/agent.go. - Basis: First-party shipped middleware and actual provider/tool/result/reinvocation closure at pinned Go source. - Confidence: High structural evidence, not an external provider or end-to-end inference claim. - Caveats: This S1=A exists in the explicitly supported installed auto-call mode. Go client does not guarantee autonomous choices if middleware/model/tool support is absent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated interference-specific feedback control among independently viable S1 units at this declared application recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing agent turns, concurrent work, inconsistent outputs and interaction conflicts could exist; the shipped multi-agent patterns do not identify and regulate a concrete inter-S1 oscillation or resource interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: NewRoundRobinGroupChatManager rotates turn index, host broadcasts messages and enforces turn/termination; caller-injected SelectNextAgent/UpdateHistory may implement custom policies, but first-party does not choose an interference-specific stabilizing policy. - Decision owner: Deterministic group host and round-robin algorithm or adopter-provided callback. The Go package owns turn scheduling, not a separate active S2 disturbance-adjustment judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Graph edges/turn tokens, concurrent fan-out, group history broadcast and manager checkpoints, sequential agents, request ports and termination guard. - Closure path: Agent output → GroupChat host updates shared history/broadcast/next turn → other agent invocation. This provides messaging and turn-taking, but lacks sensed inter-unit conflict → damping decision → adjustment fed back to multiple operating S1s. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Selecting which model agent speaks next is agent routing/turn sequencing; without an evidenced inter-S1 disturbance it does not become Viable System S2. - Evidence: workflow/agentworkflow/groupchat.go; workflow/agentworkflow/groupchat.go; workflow/agentworkflow/groupchat.go; workflow/agentworkflow/groupchat.go; workflow/agentworkflow/groupchat.go; workflow/agentworkflow/groupchat.go; workflow/agentworkflow/concurrent.go. - Basis: Direct first-party manager and host path review, bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high for packaged managers and workflow modes. - Caveats: Applications can supply a custom manager with S2-specific rules; the interface alone does not establish shipped S2 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct closed whole-current organizational manager governs multiple operational units&#39; commitments, priorities, shared resources and interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing running workflows, participant task conflicts, resource availability, constraints and obligations could require system-level current governance; no such integrated control decision was shown. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Group host chooses next chat participant and terminates a dialogue; graph checkpoint/restart and request port wake tasks. These do not decide present system-wide portfolio/resource commitments or accountability across independently purposed S1 units. - Decision owner: Per-request agent provider, developer-selected GroupChatManager or deterministic workflow runtime; no first-party accountable whole-current owner with an enforced return to separate S1 workstreams. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Host message history, manager turn state, workflow graph, checkpoints and resumption, loop evaluator iteration budget, approval pause, plan/execute mode under explicit user permission. - Closure path: Single workflow updates/broadcasts/terminates; missing aggregate operational view → current inter-unit allocation/priority/commitment choice → binding adjustment to governed S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A group conversation &#39;manager&#39; is a turn controller, not a manager of organizational survival/resource priorities. Human approval of a tool run or switching local plan/execute mode is not parent S3. - Evidence: workflow/agentworkflow/groupchat.go; workflow/agentworkflow/groupchat.go; workflow/agentworkflow/groupchat.go; workflow/checkpoint/manager.go; agent/harness/loop/loop.go; agent/harness/agentmode/agentmode.go. - Basis: Source-bound current-control vs task control distinction, bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high at app recursion. - Caveats: An adopter may implement independent S3 policy via manager callbacks/workflow, but none established in the default Go package.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary access path challenges normal current-control claims and returns corrective enforcement across S3/S1 operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfinished or low-quality model replies, unknown tool outcomes and false assertions of completion. The loop can request another response, but is a task-level feedback guard. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Installed loop.Evaluator can return Continue(feedback) and rerun, yet packaged CompletionMarkerEvaluator inspects only last model response text. Separate audit evidence and an independent corrective-management right are not present. - Decision owner: The configured evaluator callback / completion-string matcher within the same request loop; adopter would own any separately engineered verifier, not first-party default S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Evaluation structs, LastResponse/Feedback, session snapshot restart, iteration ceilings, tool-error/approval handling, tracing and workflow checkpoints. - Closure path: Agent response → completion-marker check or supplied evaluator callback → task iteration and new prompt. Lacks independent access to operating facts → challenge of S3 reporting → autonomous/legitimate corrective order into live S1 work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A feedback-triggered rerun is real, but neither checking a completion marker nor replaying one&#39;s task constitutes independently corrective complementary audit of a viable organization&#39;s current performance. - Evidence: agent/harness/loop/loop.go; agent/harness/loop/loop.go; agent/harness/loop/loop.go; agent/harness/loop/loop.go; agent/harness/loop/loop.go; agent/harness/loop/evaluators.go; agent/harness/loop/evaluators.go. - Basis: Positive test of actual built-in evaluator feedback and negative of independence/organizational audit closure. - Confidence: Medium-high at shipped first-party boundary. - Caveats: A substantive separately deployed evaluator wired through the public callback interface could create a different application-specific audit, not established here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated first-party prospective environmental intelligence generating and legitimately adopting system capability/reconfiguration choices. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Longer-horizon model/provider/tool changes, emerging external requirements and failure patterns. Session context/skills can help current tasks but no packaged future intelligence decision is closed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer supplies tools, skills, models and GroupChatManager/workflow design. agentmode supports a plan/execute permission switch explicitly directed by user rather than assessing future environment and admitting changed organization capabilities. - Decision owner: External application developer/operator for future configuration; per-request model and user for immediate plans/mode consent; no supported future capability authority returned to the runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: History/context providers, skills, plan/execute mode state, continuation, workflow checkpoint/replay, externally selected model and tool adapter. - Closure path: Configuration and session state affect present/future calls; missing environment sensing/strategic future options → authorized viability judgement → modified available capabilities/organizational configuration adopted into subsequent S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Memory, installed skills and the name &#39;plan&#39; concern current requested work, not selection and deployment of a new sustainable organizational capability. - Evidence: agent/harness/agentmode/agentmode.go; agent/harness/agentmode/agentmode.go; agent/history.go; agent/skills/skills.go; agent/agent.go; workflow/checkpoint/manager.go. - Basis: Bounded negative after testing mode switching/skills/session and workflow evolution alternatives. - Confidence: Medium-high for pinned Go package modes. - Caveats: Third-party self-modifying tools or business innovation workflows are outside assessed shipped closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No owner of final legitimate organizational policy and purpose/identity disputes is closed within the first-party Go agent application. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool policy, approval requirements, agent IDs/instructions and session modes do not establish ultimate organizational ethos or binding purpose disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human/toolapproval can permit an individual tool invocation; agentmode can switch task mode after explicit user instruction; developer registers agent Name/Description and provider config. No first-party ultimate identity arbitration is returned. - Decision owner: Adopter&#39;s human/operator and static configuration for their local tasks; not a shipped legitimate S5 governing body with durable organization-wide policy return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent configuration ID/instructions, approval-request binding, plan/execute mode permission, HITL request port and provider/tool scope. - Closure path: Current tool approval/mode/config → immediate allowed/blocked request, but absent final organizational purpose decision → authorized identity/policy change → return into system-wide governing objectives. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Operational permission and contributor/host authority are not evidence of final policy identity or resolution of irreconcilable organizational aims. - Evidence: agent/agent.go; agent/agent.go; agent/harness/toolapproval/toolapproval.go; agent/harness/agentmode/agentmode.go; workflow/requestport.go. - Basis: Shipped identity and permission surfaces reviewed, bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high for declared first-party boundary. - Caveats: External Microsoft organization, hosted Foundry service or an adopter&#39;s business may have S5, but they are not the assessed Go runtime&#39;s S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8372b7fe7ca41fdb25a9cd69613a236458dd77fd/assessments/microsoft-agent-framework-for-go.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8372b7fe7ca41fdb25a9cd69613a236458dd77fd\">8372b7f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T02:00:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lemony-ai/cascadeflow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cascadeflow</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lemony-ai/cascadeflow/tree/d8251001a51c22dc3d3ea596d89f89c4a5d74f8b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d8251001a51c22dc3d3ea596d89f89c4a5d74f8b\">d825100 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform a user/application request into a model-backed answer or tool-mediated result through cascadeflow&#39;s shipped request/routing/quality/execution loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous query complexity/domain, model/provider capability and cost, draft quality/confidence, tool requests/results, multi-turn context, and fallback/escalation conditions requiring contextual model judgment rather than a fixed literal response. - Decisive decision or feedback right: generate the operational response/tool request through the selected model-backed actor and feed that result through cascadeflow&#39;s first-party acceptance/escalation/tool-return path to determine the returned outcome. - Decision owner: the model-backed operational actor invoked through CascadeAgent&#39;s first-party provider/cascade path. cascadeflow owns the role and closure; configured providers supply external inference rather than donating unrelated metasystem functions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: complexity/domain detectors; rule/model constraints; provider adapters; WholeResponseCascade; quality validators; telemetry/cost calculation; tool normalization and ToolExecutor; request-scoped knowledge preparation; streaming/result wrappers. - Closure path: caller supplies request/messages/tools → CascadeAgent.run normalizes and routes the request → selected model-backed actor produces a draft/response or tool call → cascadeflow validates/escalates and, when configured, executes tool calls and returns their results into the loop → the final accepted model-backed result is returned to the caller with diagnostics. - Boundary reachability: CascadeAgent, provider adapters, cascade/quality routing and optional tool execution are shipped first-party runtime surfaces. A downstream developer configures models/tools but does not need to build the agentic request→model judgment→feedback→returned-result loop from generic callbacks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed actor while leaving deterministic routing, telemetry and limit machinery preserves bookkeeping but removes the contextual judgment that produces the primary answer/tool decision. S1 discretion is therefore agent-owned even though the inference provider itself is external. - Evidence: README.md; cascadeflow/agent.py; cascadeflow/core/cascade.py; cascadeflow/tools/executor.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external LangChain/CrewAI/OpenAI Agents/OpenClaw/Hermes/etc. agent loops are not credited. S1 is limited to cascadeflow&#39;s own model-backed CascadeAgent role and closure at the frozen revision.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party same-recursion inter-S1 coordination function was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established because the standard assessed recursion exposes one cascadeflow operational unit; multiple model calls, drafter/verifier stages and parallel tool calls do not by themselves create distinct same-recursion S1 units with an evidenced mutual disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S2. - Decision owner: not established as a first-party S2 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model routing; drafter→verifier sequencing; tool routing; parallel tool execution; integration adapters; multi-agent examples. - Closure path: not applicable at S2; no specific inter-S1 conflict/oscillation → coordination response → changed subsequent S1 behaviour loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: cascadeflow can route among models and can be embedded into external multi-agent frameworks, but routing/delegation is not S2 without distinct S1 units plus a concrete interference witness and attenuation path. - Evidence: cascadeflow/agent.py; cascadeflow/core/cascade.py; cascadeflow/tools/executor.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external framework integrations may participate in S2 at the host framework&#39;s recursion, but their coordination authority is outside the declared cascadeflow boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate the current cascadeflow-managed run on behalf of the whole run by maintaining aggregate operating state and applying current resource/constraint interventions to subsequent model/tool activity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cumulative cost can exhaust budget; tool calls can exceed a run limit; latency/energy can exceed bounds; compliance policy can make a model/tool path inadmissible; KPI pressure can make another model preferable while the current run is still executing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: on each intercepted model call, choose allow, switchmodel, denytool, or stop from current aggregate run state and configured constraints, then apply that transition in enforce mode. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic constructor/control logic in harness/instrument.py, parameterized by developer/operator configuration. No autonomous supervisory agent owns the current-control discretion in the evidenced standard mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HarnessRunContext counters/limits; OpenAI/Anthropic instrumentation; model price/latency/energy priors; compliance allowlists; KPI weights; trace recording; exceptions; model/tool argument mutation. - Closure path: model/tool activity updates the scoped run totals → before the next model call, evaluateprecalldecision reads those current totals/constraints → resolveprecalldecision applies switch/deny/stop/allow in enforce mode → the next operation is changed or terminated → subsequent totals feed the next control decision. - Boundary reachability: init(mode=&quot;enforce&quot;), run(...), HarnessRunContext and the built-in instrumentation/pre-call decision path are shipped together and directly applied to supported instrumented provider calls. A downstream user chooses constraints but does not need to invent the current-state aggregation or enforcement feedback path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing any autonomous model while keeping the run-state and instrumentation machinery leaves materially the same budget/tool/latency/energy/compliance/KPI control decision. The current-control function is therefore real but not agent-owned; the published constructor/control state is C. - Evidence: README.md; cascadeflow/harness/api.py; cascadeflow/harness/instrument.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: configuration authors own the policy values; deterministic enforcement does not turn them into an autonomous S3 actor. Conversely, the path is more than telemetry alone because the shipped enforce mode feeds the decision back into subsequent current operation. - Whole-system current view: the scoped HarnessRunContext aggregates cost, tool calls, latency, energy, budget remaining, model/action state and configured run constraints across the current cascadeflow-managed run. - Current-control decision scope: whether the next intercepted model call may proceed, which model may serve it, whether tool capability remains available, or whether the current run must stop under the configured resource/compliance/KPI envelope.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially independent complementary audit path was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cascadeflow validates draft quality and can call a verifier, but these checks are routine production-path acceptance/escalation stages rather than sporadic/complementary access to operational reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as a separate audit judgment. - Decision owner: not established as a first-party S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QualityValidator; drafter/verifier cascade; semantic alignment/confidence checks; telemetry/traces; tests and examples. - Closure path: not applicable at S3; the located checks remain in the ordinary response-production path or adjacent development/evaluation surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a verifier model may independently generate a response, but routine verifier use inside the normal cascade does not by itself satisfy complementary-audit independence or alternative access. - Evidence: cascadeflow/core/cascade.py; cascadeflow/quality/quality.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host application could use cascadeflow traces/results inside an external audit organization; that external composition is not shipped S3 closure for cascadeflow itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: adaptive quality thresholds react to internal acceptance history, but no shipped loop was found that models changing external users/regulation/threats/dependencies/opportunities, develops future-oriented organizational options, and returns such options to present capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S4. - Decision owner: not established as a first-party S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AdaptiveThresholdManager; rolling outcome windows; hard-query embeddings; domain detection; runtime configuration update APIs; telemetry forecasting/degradation utilities. - Closure path: not applicable at S4; internal operating outcomes can tune later quality thresholds, but that is generic learning/current optimization rather than outside-and-then adaptation closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-adjusting thresholds are algorithmic learning from internal production history. The Profile explicitly requires external/future distinctions and adaptation-option development, which the inspected path does not supply. - Evidence: cascadeflow/quality/adaptive.py; cascadeflow/quality/quality.py; cascadeflow/agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future model/provider selection can change as thresholds adjust, but temporal effect alone is not S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: budget, compliance, KPI weights, model allowlists, routing rules and tool restrictions constrain execution, but they are ordinary operating policy/configuration rather than a path for deciding the organization&#39;s ultimate identity or supreme policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S5. - Decision owner: not established as a first-party S5 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: harness config; compliance allowlists; KPI/routing rules; tenant/channel overrides; model/tool restrictions; environment/file configuration. - Closure path: not applicable at S5; no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces configured constraints and can optimize model choice, but neither an autonomous actor nor a parent-governed shipped path is given ultimate identity/policy decision authority. - Evidence: cascadeflow/harness/api.py; cascadeflow/harness/instrument.py; cascadeflow/rules/engine.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an operator can change configuration, but generic ability to edit policy values is not a qualifying parent S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0a0386b00bfba01ee71b4b17fbe2d5ca34c09ca8/assessments/cascadeflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0a0386b00bfba01ee71b4b17fbe2d5ca34c09ca8\">0a0386b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-25T00:49:53+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Protocol-Lattice/go-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">go-agent</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-25\">2026-09-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Protocol-Lattice/go-agent/tree/ddfec8630783306b4ecb5ffd37519595384f8d15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ddfec8630783306b4ecb5ffd37519595384f8d15\">ddfec86 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute requests through a model-driven loop that can select and invoke first-party-registered tools/sub-agents, observe results and continue until substantive completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous user objectives, changing tool observations, external API/file results, failed/invalid plans, memory/context variation and tasks that require multiple environment interactions before completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether to call another tool, which exact tool and arguments to use, and when the request is complete; configured direct sub-agent paths provide additional first-party operating choices. - Decision owner: the model-driven Agent planner within the first-party runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory retrieval, tool catalog, UTCP execution, sub-agent directory, CodeMode restrictions, input/output guardrails, duplicate-call handling, mutation-completion checks and configured step limits. - Closure path: request/context enter Agent → model planner selects a tool/action or completion → first-party runtime executes the selected registered tool → observation returns into the planner context → model selects the next action or final answer → result is stored/returned. - Boundary reachability: Agent.Generate and its normal tool-orchestration path are shipped core package behavior; ADK builds the same first-party Agent runtime rather than delegating the loop to an external harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime checks constrain the allowed action space and termination envelope, but they do not determine which substantive tool/arguments or follow-up action satisfy the request. Removing the model planner removes those decisions. - Evidence: agent.go; agenttoolorchestration.go; src/adk/kit.go. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: direct deterministic tool commands and caller-defined graph nodes also exist. A rests on the standard reachable model-owned operating path, not on every execution mode being autonomous.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 disturbance attenuation loop is established for the declared standard runtime boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sub-agent directories, agent-as-tool composition, shared-session spaces, swarm participant join/leave, graph fan-out/join and ordinary delegation were inspected as supporting mechanisms only. - Closure path: no first-party disturbance-specific S2 closure established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the framework can instantiate and connect multiple autonomous agents, but the caller defines their roles/topology. Shared memory transports information; graph joins collect outputs; sub-agent calls delegate work. None of these standard mechanisms specifies a concrete cross-S1 oscillation/conflict/resource-interference problem and a coordination relation whose feedback changes later S1 behavior. - Evidence: src/swarm/swarm.go; src/swarm/participant.go; src/memory/session/sharedsession.go; src/adk/kit.go; src/adk/workflow/graph.go. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: a downstream organization built with go-agent may establish strong S2. That separately instantiated organization requires its own evidence and does not donate S2 to the reusable framework assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system current-management function is established at the declared runtime boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at whole-system scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic graph queues/checkpoints, model middleware rate/token/timeout policies, per-request tool-loop limits, guardrails and caller-configured coordinator/sub-agent relationships were inspected. - Closure path: no whole-system current-view → discretionary current-control decision → changed commitments/resources/priorities loop is packaged. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: graph execution follows caller-defined edges/routes and persists one run&#39;s queue/state; model middleware enforces configured limits around calls/workflows; a default prompt calling an agent a “primary coordinator” does not supply a current whole-system view or management authority. - Evidence: src/adk/workflow/graph.go; README.md; src/adk/kit.go. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: applications can construct a manager agent or current-control service from these primitives. Such downstream construction is a different organizational boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary operational audit path is established for ordinary go-agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Arena evaluators, output guardrails, LLM safety evaluator, tests and tracing/tool events were inspected. - Closure path: no independent complementary-access findings → corrective current-operation return path is packaged. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Arena deliberately separates benchmark execution from evaluation but evaluates the supplied task/output contract and records score/feedback; it is not wired into ordinary runtime control and does not obtain an independent view of operational reality. Output guardrails inspect the ordinary proposed response in-path before return, which is routine QA rather than complementary audit. - Evidence: arena/arena.go; arena/evaluators.go; safetypolicies.go. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party prospective organizational/capability adaptation loop is established at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an outside/future adaptation function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent memory, local skills, Skill System 2.0 evaluator hooks/persistence, workspace indexing/watching, provider/tool discovery and checkpointing were inspected. - Closure path: no first-party external/future distinction → generated/selected adaptation option → approved revision → returned future capability loop is packaged. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Skill System 2.0 can evaluate a skill, persist a caller-supplied SkillDefinition with SaveSkill, load skills and route them into future prompts, but the framework does not connect evaluation/external evidence to an actor that generates or selects a revised skill. The adopter must build that missing adaptation decision/feedback organization. Workspace Intelligence tracks current repository changes to keep context fresh; it does not adapt the harness&#39;s own future repertoire. - Evidence: skillsv2.go; skillruntime.go; docs/skill-system-2.md; docs/workspace-intelligence.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established for the declared runtime boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at identity/ultimate-policy scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configurable system prompt/default coordinator prompt, safety policies, input/output guardrails, tool permissions, skills and caller configuration were inspected. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → returned identity/policy decision loop is packaged. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts, guardrails and permissions constrain ordinary operation, but the framework does not distinguish a durable organizational identity/ultimate-policy source or an authority process that resolves such issues and returns the result into operation. - Evidence: agent.go; src/adk/kit.go; safetypolicies.go. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/48fdf81a686c7f003288383196352049e08519da/assessments/go-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 48fdf81a686c7f003288383196352049e08519da\">48fdf81 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T11:31:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI Maestro</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/tree/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165\">6c8a592 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute independently scoped project tasks and produce model-chosen code, research or operational output. - Disturbance / variety regulated: source changes, requirements, test failures and external messages for an agent&#39;s owned repository/process. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the active external CLI model decides what tools to use and how to revise its work. - Decision owner: attached Claude Code/Codex/other supported CLI model agent; AI Maestro supplies runtime access, message and context transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tmux/remote session management, per-agent clone, tasks, AMP, memory and tool approvals. - Closure path: operator/task brief → model agent session acts in own working directory → changed artifact/test result → agent reports via message/task board. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the dashboard can display/send work but does not generate the actual task-specific code decisions without its model agent. - Evidence: https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/README.md; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/lib/agent-runtime.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agents-core-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agents-chat-service.ts - Basis: shipped CLI integration and task communication path; no live external LLM E2E. - Confidence: high for supported S1 actor boundary, unmeasured outcomes. - Caveats: some supported terminal programs have fewer chat/control integrations; no third-party provider internals are credited. - Boundary reachability: standard deployed agent session on local tmux or supported remote host.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no qualifying first-party inter-S1 incompatible work arbitration or repair path was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two independent agents may change the same API or repository branch incompatibly. - Decisive decision or feedback right: humans/agents choose how to respond to AMP messages; the service only delivers messages and tracks task blockers. - Decision owner: no deployed S2 controller with a specific conflict-detection/settlement right demonstrated; agents retain local task choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent clone/branch isolation, signed AMP transport, meeting delivery statuses, blockedBy graph and waiting cards. - Closure path: agents see messages/blocked status and may respond; no shipped concrete incompatible-artifact detection and corrective return to each S1 was verified. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: AMP, org/task links and Git clone separation transport or prevent some overlap but do not prove actual cross-S1 correction judgment. - Evidence: https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agents-messaging-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/meeting-broadcast-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/lib/task-registry.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/docs/CONCEPTS.md - Basis: bounded negative across message, task dependency and Git ownership surfaces. - Confidence: medium; human or agent teams may build effective coordination practices outside this tested first-party circuit. - Caveats: dependency completion computes newly unblocked IDs but does not itself merge incompatible code, enforce dependent execution or negotiate changed interfaces.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: let a parent human regulate present distributed team work, current priorities, assignments and blocked commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: many agents across hosts, blocked or overdue tasks, limited operator attention, delivery failures and work waiting for approval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator chooses which agent owns a card, current priority/status, instructions, meeting scope and approval response from team-wide present state. - Decision owner: parent human operator acting through installed dashboard and chat, not a default AI Maestro management model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: host/agent status aggregation, Matrix/Attention board, task API, chat-to-terminal delivery, team meeting and receipt/status records. - Closure path: team-wide active/blocked/waiting view → human assignment or intervention → persisted task update or message/approval delivery → agent receives new current commitment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: UI and API do not choose priorities or reconcile across team autonomously; present controlling judgment remains with user (P). - Evidence: https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/lib/organization.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agent-tasks-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agents-chat-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/meeting-broadcast-service.ts - Basis: supported parent-assisted operating mode with real whole-team view and task/message write paths. - Confidence: medium-high for available human-controlled current-team closure; individual user&#39;s actual meeting choices are not observed. - Caveats: no automatic model S3 is claimed; unattended API usage alone and task board visuals alone do not prove the human actually exercises every S3 judgment. - Whole-system current view: Matrix/Attention aggregates open, waiting, review and blocked cards across agents and hosts; organization view groups active work and assignments. - Current-control decision scope: assign/change task owner, choose present priority and current commitments, send corrective instruction or answer a pending agent approval. - Boundary reachability: normal self-hosted dashboard and integrated task/chat API directly expose operator write actions to working agent sessions.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party sufficiently independent alternative audit of work outcomes and return to control was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker could falsely report completion or produce a regression unobserved by routine status. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator may review a card/commit trace, but no separately invoked independent challenger is mandated or shown. - Decision owner: no specific S3 judge for actual work evidence within assessed code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review column, commit trace, agent chat history, docs/code graph and activity status. - Closure path: visible trace can lead a human to inspect manually, without a packaged complementary auditor diagnosis and correction route. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: passive trace, status and ordinary review labels do not choose adverse fault findings. - Evidence: https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/README.md; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/commit-trace-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agent-tasks-service.ts - Basis: bounded negative across review statuses, session tracing and docs/code index. - Confidence: medium; an external agent role could provide audit when explicitly installed elsewhere. - Caveats: a screenshot of a code-reviewer agent is not proof of its executable independent audit or return loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective environmental options process was wired back into the operator&#39;s present team capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future customers, infrastructure changes, agent models and emerging threats may require adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: local memory and scheduled agent tasks collect/contextualize history; no S4 options owner is shown. - Decision owner: human leadership for future strategy, if supplied outside this app. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: long-term memory, entity graph, agent schedules, code graph and reusable notes. - Closure path: learned retrospective context reaches later prompts, but no external prospective option/choice → S3 capability change loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory consolidation and nightly summaries are retrospective operational support, not outside-and-then policy intelligence. - Evidence: https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/lib/memory/consolidate.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agents-schedule-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/README.md - Basis: bounded negative on memory, schedules, organization and information surfaces. - Confidence: medium-high at published runtime boundary. - Caveats: an operator can program a strategic research agent; that external configuration is not first-party S4 provenance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity-level ultimate policy court or balance of current and future organizational identity is implemented. - Disturbance / variety regulated: company labels, agent roles, secret permissions, approval and conflicting objectives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator can label organizations and approve individual secrets/actions; there is no identity-level dispute resolution path. - Decision owner: human company authority external to installed model worker ensemble; no evidenced packaged S5 issue closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: org tags and names, agent roles, permission/secret UI, messages and dashboard. - Closure path: task approval or org rename affects local work/display, but no ultimate policy ruling is returned to a sustained S3/S4 decision relation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an organization record is explicitly shown, not enforced; a role label or approval does not constitute S5. - Evidence: https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/lib/organization.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/orgs-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agents-chat-service.ts - Basis: bounded negative review of org, identity, role, permission and approval surfaces. - Confidence: high that labels alone are not governance; external human policy remains outside boundary. - Caveats: actual human-run companies may have real S5, but they are not inherited by this software product.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/57b15cceb7ca319c1f0aa55399f182700a3c1255/assessments/ai/ai-maestro.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 57b15cceb7ca319c1f0aa55399f182700a3c1255\">57b15cc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T00:59:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shareAI-lab/kode-agent-sdk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KODE SDK</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shareAI-lab/kode-agent-sdk/tree/2596118805d8968c7e33cc437e440721833e9689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2596118805d8968c7e33cc437e440721833e9689\">2596118 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute a supplied task through an autonomous model-backed operational actor using offered tools and feedback from their effects. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unpredictable user input, sandbox contents, tool errors, permission outcomes and intermediate model responses require choosing what to do next. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The external model selects a next tool call or content response using the context and advertised schemas; subsequent tool observations influence the next model request. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed Agent operational work cell. The model provider supplies inference; first-party SDK code deliberately constructs and closes its model/tool/observation loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry/runner, PermissionManager, sandbox, context compression, stores, message queue, hooks, events, scheduling and operator-configured limits. - Closure path: Agent.complete/chat → queued prompt → runStep → model.stream → tool-use selection → executeTools → tool outcomes appended as user messages → ensureProcessing/next model turn or completed answer. - Boundary reachability: Agent is a public SDK export and its normal complete mode directly invokes the agent loop; no example-only controller or evaluation job is needed to receive and act on model-chosen tool calls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without model discretion, the existing deterministic transport, retries, approvals and tool runner cannot decide which task-specific tool to invoke next. The model&#39;s tool selection is operational ownership, not merely its ability to produce text. - Evidence: public Agent export; complete and send; stream and closure; delegated actor completion. - Basis: Structural first-party runtime call path and published API. - Confidence: High for implemented autonomous decision/feedback; no empirical claim of effectiveness or safety. - Caveats: The SDK requires external model credentials/dependencies and user-provided task/template. A child Agent is a bounded operational unit, not automatic proof of recursive viability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party coordination circuit specifically attenuating interference, collision or oscillation between two distinct S1 work cells is evidenced in the standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Separate agents could potentially disagree, duplicate edits or collide in one sandbox, but the SDK does not represent a concrete shared-work conflict and a conflict-specific response. - Distinct S1 units: AgentPool holds multiple independent model-backed Agent instances, and Room delivers messages to selected members; this establishes multiplicity, not S2 closure. - Inter-S1 disturbance: No collision detection, reservation ownership, contradictory-output reconciliation, conflict arbitration or feedback from a detected peer disturbance is represented in the reviewed multi-agent primitives. - Attenuating coordination relation: Room.say transfers text to one or more peers; taskrun serially delegates a parent task to a child. Neither checks or dampens any particular inter-agent interference. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Recipient models may condition their next output on delivered messages, but there is no coordination result from an inter-S1 instability being fed back under a first-party S2 protocol. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The functions inspected are precisely ordinary transport, pool lifecycle and delegation. An embedding app could add a dispute procedure, but none is installed by KODE SDK itself. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developers/operators decide topology and communication content; no first-party S2 response-selection or conflict-resolution owner is established. - Decision owner: No qualifying S2 owner inside this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Room membership map, mentions/broadcast, pool capacity checks, subagent depth/template restrictions, caller-defined coordinator system prompt. - Closure path: Parent invokes child and awaits result, or Room.say calls peer complete; neither closes a specifically evidenced conflict → mitigation → revised S1 behavior relation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent-originated task messages may be discretionary, but autonomy of message content does not make generic delivery an S2 function. - Evidence: Room; pool; taskrun tool; documented pattern is user-authored. - Basis: Bounded negative on shipped first-party multi-agent path. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An application&#39;s own scripted negotiation, locking or conflict-aware shared-work layer would move the assessed boundary; no such application is included here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No bundled whole-current regulation of the active agent population&#39;s conflicting commitments, shared-resource allocation or accountability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agents could exhaust concurrent capacity, exceed context limits or produce weak work, but the SDK exposes fixed caps and local agent processes, not a controller that evaluates and revises the whole&#39;s priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The embedding developer chooses which agents to create and how to distribute tasks; AgentPool.maxAgents is a configured guard, not a model-owned or constructor-managed choice among current commitments. - Decision owner: External embedding application/developer for population design; the SDK has no qualified S3 owner or parent-return circuit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pool maxAgents, task delegation, per-agent todos, context compression, step scheduler, event monitor, graceful shutdown and resume. - Closure path: Fixed configured limits reject additional creations; shutdown persists/interrupts operations; subagent results return to their parent task. No whole-population appraisal → discretionary resource/priorities revision → changed S1 operations exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A coordinator-like prompt in docs may choose delegates inside its own task; without an installed whole-current view and authority over the other S1 units, delegation is S1 decomposition rather than S3. Hard-coded capacity cannot own choices about commitments. - Evidence: pool create and maxAgents; pool shutdown; local scheduler; parent task delegation. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking S3 constructor and parent possibilities. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A self-hosted app could place an autonomous manager over this SDK; that manager is not a first-party supported control mode established in the package.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent inspection of claims by operating agents beyond their ordinary output/tool/event stream is part of this SDK&#39;s working governance loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent results might be inaccurate, unauthorized or misleading; permission checks and event logs are local execution governance rather than independent raw-reality audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separately authorized audit finding about operational reports is made from complementary evidence and fed into current-control decisions. - Decision owner: Operator may inspect events or build a reviewer agent in an application; the SDK itself does not construct independent audit ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Progress/control/monitor event channels, approval decisions, tool-result records, generic reviewer template documented in examples, snapshot/replay facilities. - Closure path: Tool results reenter the same producer&#39;s model context; event subscribers and human approvals can affect routine work but have no independent audit judgment returning to an S3 regulator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A second model named reviewer in a user template is normal production QA until the independently sourced evidence, auditor ownership and return to separate control are demonstrated. - Evidence: agent event and tool-result path; event bus; optional reviewer example. - Basis: Bounded negative standard-distribution review. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Independent tests or reviewers implemented by a downstream application can change the result at that application&#39;s boundary; such a mode is not credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party future-oriented external-environment sensing → adaptation option → adoption into current operating capability circuit is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future shifts in models, tools or workloads might require adaptation, but the shipping runtime handles current conversations and lets an operator manage skills and templates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The SDK does not choose and adopt future capability alternatives from external intelligence; operators/developers install skills and update model/templates outside a closed agentic S4 loop. - Decision owner: Developer/operator for versions, providers and skill sets; per-task agent/tool choices remain S1. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Hot skill scanning, SkillsManagementManager import/archive/export, model provider configuration, context compression, hooks and lifecycle events. - Closure path: Changed skill files may become visible on the next scan, but no controller has sensed a prospective environmental need, compared adaptation options and returned a chosen capability change to current work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Skill file CRUD and hot reload are mechanisms for carrying a user-selected change. Tool learning/context management during a present task is not a prospective organizational adaptation loop. - Evidence: skill runtime scan; separate skill-management service and explicit no-runtime scope; agent context compression. - Basis: Bounded negative with developer-managed future adaptation possibility distinguished from shipped S4. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An external agent using SDK tools to research future providers and update skills could create an S4 path in a larger system, but no such first-party feedback circuit is wired into this standard runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate identity/purpose-policy deliberation with legitimate returned binding governance is evidenced for the installed agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool safety and permission conflicts are handled as routine task-level policy constraints, not identity-level disputes between present execution and future adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A developer sets permission mode and allowed tools; an external user can approve/deny a pending tool call through agent.decide. Neither is a runtime ultimate-policy question with organizational deliberation and returned identity governance. - Decision owner: Developer/application operator for configured constraints and individual API approval; no qualified first-party S5 agent or parent identity authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionManager allow/deny/ask, mode handlers, system prompt, agent.decide, sandbox configuration, tool registration and agent template. - Closure path: Pending tool request → ordinary user/API permission decision → resume or deny the tool. This closes a single operational authorization, not a policy/identity issue with legitimate parent decision and return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model must obey runtime permission decisions, but these are preconfigured or call-by-call; a restrictive system prompt or human approval does not itself implement S5. - Evidence: permission evaluation; API permission resolution; template-based constraints. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking parent-governed and autonomous S5 paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The external hosting application&#39;s human owner may legitimately supply S5 in a broader organization, but that application&#39;s identity-policy escalation and return are not installed with the SDK.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/dc66c814c10a69e4f5d12ef33ae4c0be72bd400d/assessments/ko/kode-sdk.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit dc66c814c10a69e4f5d12ef33ae4c0be72bd400d\">dc66c81 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T15:34:41+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/moazbuilds/CodeMachine-CLI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CodeMachine CLI</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/moazbuilds/CodeMachine-CLI/tree/572def63eb808e95b18ccf6c69a13d7a13fe06fd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 572def63eb808e95b18ccf6c69a13d7a13fe06fd\">572def6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute discretionary coding/workflow instructions in a real workspace through configured model-backed coding agents and return their outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing codebase state and task intent, tool errors, provider availability, multi-step coding goals and responses from the workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The invoked coding-engine agent interprets the current task, chooses modifications/tool steps inside its own engine loop and responds to observations; CodeMachine&#39;s runtime launches and resumes that specific actor through a first-party installed command path. - Decision owner: Installed model-backed coding-work cells connected by CodeMachine runner; the cognition and inner tool loop are external-provider machinery, while first-party CodeMachine owns their task issuance, runtime integration and result/session return. Provider internals are not silently credited as first-party metasystems. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent templates, combined prompts, engine registry/auth cache/fallback, MCP tool context, abort/timeout, monitoring IDs, JSON event/status store, chained prompts, process stdout/session callbacks. - Closure path: CLI or TUI goal → first-party coordinator/step loader → executeAgent and external engine.run → model-backed tool/observation loop → output/status/session record → result or subsequent chained workflow step. - Boundary reachability: registerRunCommand calls CoordinatorService.execute; its executor directly calls executeAgent, which resolves and calls a real configured engine; the shipped workflow runner independently uses the same method. This is not only an abstract interface or test fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: If model-backed coding engines are removed, authentication checks, parser, prompt files, static workflow gates and logs remain, but no autonomous coding decisions or tool-mediated progress. Their internal intelligence is a dependency, yet the assembled first-party agent-operating mode is real. - Evidence: runnable CLI; execution groups; engine selection, run and result. - Basis: Structural deployed standard CLI/TUI path, assuming a supported coding CLI is configured. - Confidence: High for integration of the real agent executable; no source-independent performance assertion. - Caveats: Vendor model/tool loops and authorization are external; no live model execution was used in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party conflict-specific regulation between distinct S1 work cells is established for the standard local service recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel coding agents can edit overlapping workspace files or disagree about interfaces; the observed framework supports parallel/sequential work but no shipped collision detector, joint resource reservation or model negotiation response specific to such a disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The user-written coordination DSL fixes parallel versus sequential execution; CoordinationExecutor starts and joins tasks, halts sequential groups on failure and reports status. These do not decide how to harmonize two conflicting agents or return corrective agreements to both. - Decision owner: Host-specified script/template order and deterministic group executor; possible project-specific coordination by spawned external coding agents is not a first-party shipped S2 relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CoordinatorParser, group mode, Promise.all, stop-on-error, inherited parent-agent IDs, monitor registry, workflow statuses and directive fields. - Closure path: A group&#39;s tasks yield results which advance or stop later groups. A concrete detected cross-agent interference and corresponding coordination outcome returned to modify both work cells is not shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: CoordinatorService naming, hierarchy, fan-out and stream ordering are scheduling/delegation. Potential file collisions in a shared workspace are not themselves evidence that CodeMachine ships a collision-attentuation path. - Evidence: scripted parallel/sequential groups; parent/child monitoring; workflow status/sequence. - Basis: Bounded negative under the Profile S2 inter-S1 disturbance and specific feedback test. - Confidence: Medium-high for standard shipped paths. - Caveats: A downstream project could implement collision-specific review/locks with custom agents and templates; the generic constructor alone does not qualify for C.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No installed discretionary whole-current control over cross-agent resource commitments, priorities or performance at the CodeMachine service recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed coding agents, stalled sessions, excessive runtime and poor step output could warrant management; shipped records expose status and allow stop/resume, but the reviewed decisions remain fixed/session-local or human-preconfigured. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured scripts/templates choose step order; per-step directives may rewind a preset number of steps and a controller can converse with the user before execution. Neither source establishes a manager seeing all current operations and adaptively bargaining/reallocating their commitments as a whole. - Decision owner: Host/human for template/controller start, fixed directive/loop code for step retry/rewind, external engines for local coding choices. No first-party agent-owned S3 current-management mode or distinct operationally closed parent S3 loop is shown. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Monitoring DB/active agents, telemetry, abort/timeout, StatusService, step index manager, preset maxIterations, directive.json and human continue/checkpoint. - Closure path: A step may write an enabled loop directive, causing configured previous steps to re-execute; a checkpoint waits for human continue/quit. This lacks evidence that one current whole-system view justifies a managerial decision over resource/commitment allocation across the ongoing S1 population. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hard loop and fail-stop controls change execution order but do not alone supply the S3-wide accountability/right; user pre-execution planning is goal setup, not the whole-now managerial loop. - Evidence: controller pre-execution conversation/continue; loop directive evaluation; rewind flow; runner status/engine fallback. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking strongest candidate (stepBack and controller). - Confidence: Medium: externally composed workflows can become strong management systems, but are not evidenced in this standard boundary. - Caveats: Loop/rewind is not ignored; it is an explicit first-party step-control mechanism, lacking a proven whole-current S3 decision-right witness and therefore not promoted merely by naming.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent alternative reading of S1 results and audit-finding return into S3 current control is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An external coding agent might falsely report success; logs, telemetry and retryable steps expose artifacts but remain normal self/runner reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Monitoring stores provider stdout, status and telemetry. Users may inspect logs, and custom workflow steps could review code, but no shipped complementary evaluator with independent raw evidence and correction-return authority is installed. - Decision owner: Runner/monitor records ordinary agent execution; optional operator review and downstream custom reviewer agents are outside a demonstrated built-in S3 channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Monitoring DB, log snapshots, session IDs, status graph, checkpoints and telemetry dashboards. - Closure path: Logs go to UI/CLI; failure statuses can halt steps. No independent sampled replay/ground-truth comparison is routed to a distinct S3 decision-right to correct first-party agent claims. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A transcript and a hypothetical review step are not themselves complementary operational evidence or independence. - Evidence: normal runner log/telemetry and status; coordinator results; human checkpoint. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high for public shipped operating modes. - Caveats: A project can embed an independent auditor in an agent template, but it must be evidenced on its own runtime boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No recurring prospective external intelligence that develops adaptations and returns accepted changes into the harness&#39;s current operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tools, provider models, software requirements or architecture changes may demand future evolution; available workflow builder serves a user-requested task rather than a standing capability-governance cycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator selects provider/model/imports and can ask Ali to design a workflow; no first-party S4 model chooses a capability migration based on ongoing environmental monitoring and returns it to live S3 operations. - Decision owner: Operator/configured downstream developer; currently invoked model-backed builder owns its S1 design output, not organizational S4 adaptation of CodeMachine as a whole. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent/config imports, Ali builder prompts, model fallback, template registries, runtime configuration and telemetry. - Closure path: A user can generate or select a new workflow; source does not show a standing outside-oriented sensing → evaluated adaptation options → incorporation into present operational control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A design/code-generation work product or a change of selected engine is not prospective organizational intelligence closure by default. - Evidence: shipped Ali builder configuration; imports and chosen template; engine fallback. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: User-constructed workflows may develop future options for their external organization; that is a separate system/recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate-identity dispute escalation, legitimate authority decision and binding return loop is installed in a CodeMachine service instance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model/tool permissions and workflows require consent, but those are ordinary operational safety envelopes, not observed identity-level policy conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User approves or pauses controller/workflow steps, chooses agent templates and model configuration; agents can request checkpoint/stop. No source route treats a foundational purpose/policy dispute as S5 and installs the resulting authoritative rule for subsequent work. - Decision owner: Human operator sets local permissions, goals and tools externally; model actors decide bounded coding tasks. No evidenced S5 ultimate-policy holder is part of an operational closure path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Interactive controller gate, checkpoint/quit, MCP filter context, engine auth, static config, permission-related vendor flags and directive constraints. - Closure path: Human continue transitions from planning to execution or lets a checkpoint pass. Neither is an identity-policy deliberation returned as durable binding governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A controller role, prompt instructions and user approval of an ordinary step cannot be promoted to S5 on names alone. - Evidence: controller continue gate; checkpoint confirmation; MCP context / auth envelope. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Downstream development organization might impose its own ultimate policy, but it is not automatically a closed CodeMachine product S5 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a22b4005f08acbf62e0bfc4f3e92fc4e49b79ef6/assessments/co/codemachine-cli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a22b4005f08acbf62e0bfc4f3e92fc4e49b79ef6\">a22b400 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T00:44:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/go-kratos/blades\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Blades</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/go-kratos/blades/tree/b78fec59029d11e02c135ea10da8986ed006521f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b78fec59029d11e02c135ea10da8986ed006521f\">b78fec5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Handle current tasks through model-selected operations and tool execution, retaining actual observations for further model decisions and user output. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended user prompts, multimodal inputs, changes in tool/API responses, errors, session context and subtasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Within the first-party agent.handle loop, the configured model selects tools/parameters or a final message based on prior history; tool execution returns actual observations into the next model request. - Decision owner: Configured model-directed Agent operating within Blades&#39; first-party loop. The remote model host is a provider dependency, while middleware/iteration limits and developer registrations constrain the agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: NewRunner, Session history, Invocation data, Tool handler, middleware, optional skill/tool resolution, maxIterations, message streaming and tool-exit signals. - Closure path: Input Runner.Run → agent Run/prepareInvocation → provider Generate or NewStreaming → RoleTool → executeTools/actual tool Handle result → session/local message history → next Generate or completed assistant answer. - Boundary reachability: NewAgent configures a concrete Agent.Run, not just an interface sketch; Runner actually calls it, and handle executes registered tools and re-enters provider decisions for both stateless and session-history modes. Optional NewDeepAgent returns an actual configured NewAgent with task/todo tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model decides next action from returned tool evidence; the deterministic tool handler, middleware and session are subordinate execution and data mechanisms. This is distinct from a purely prewritten graph sequence. - Evidence: agent.go; agent.go; agent.go; agent.go; agent.go; agent.go; agent.go; runner.go. - Basis: Explicit reachable source showing model/tool/observation/next-decision closure. - Confidence: High for first-party source structure; no external model/provider inference executed. - Caveats: Actual provider integration, tools and policy are supplied at deployment; task-level autonomy does not imply metasystem autonomy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No verified first-party inter-S1 disturbance-specific damping relation with returned mutual adjustment is installed at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel subagent contention, inconsistent data writes and competing tool actions could occur; actual inter-unit conflict-response policy is not implemented in the reviewed flow primitives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ParallelAgent starts and streams peers; RoutingAgent selects a named recipient; LoopAgent enforces configured iteration/condition. None detects a specific organizational disturbance between independently viable S1 cells and authoritatively attenuates it. - Decision owner: Task model router, programmatic workflow composer and deterministic Go flow execution; no S2-specific mutual-coordination decision owner evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Subagent clone/errgroup, cancellation, HandoffTool routing, loop exit, graph dependency scheduling, Session and nested task tool. - Closure path: Current task routing/parallel result/loop outputs reach the invoker; missing independent S1 conflict observation → S2-specific stabilization choice → enforced adjustments returned to affected distinct S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concurrent agents, cancellation and model-chosen handoff are useful orchestration, not automatically S2; no concrete disturbance-specific reciprocal dampening is established. - Evidence: flow/parallel.go; flow/routing.go; flow/loop.go; flow/deep.go; internal/deep/tasktool.go; graph/executor.go. - Basis: Bounded negative after explicit inspection of supported first-party flow/graph modes. - Confidence: Medium-high for source-inspected shipped paths. - Caveats: An adopter could supply a separate S2 algorithm via callbacks; hypothetical composition does not upgrade shipped framework.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No app-wide current-control system is evidenced that integrates several S1 operating units and makes binding resource/commitment/priority interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential multiple subagent workloads, resource exhaustion, deadlines and competing workflow commitments; actual aggregate management is not present in the inspected first-party decision path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: DeepAgent can model-select task delegation and update todos for its current task; Runner runs a root agent, and graph scheduler advances configured tasks. Neither represents a whole-current organizational command right across independent S1 purposes/commitments. - Decision owner: Task agent, developer-authored graph/flow configuration, deterministic Runner/graph executor. No S3 metasystem owner or legitimate parent return path proven. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskTool, WriteTodos, graph checkpoint/recovery, session tracking, max iterations, parallel cancellation and optional middleware. - Closure path: Current task → todo/delegation → feedback → task completion; absent whole-system current view → strategic present resource/priority/commitment adjudication → adjustment returned to distinct S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parent-subagent dispatch for one goal is S1 decomposition, not necessarily S3 management of an enduring organization; a checkpoint or todo list does not confer a S3 right. - Evidence: flow/deep.go; internal/deep/tasktool.go; internal/deep/writetodostool.go; runner.go; graph/executor.go; graph/executor.go. - Basis: Explicit current-work context/graph/delegation inspection; bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high at the declared application recursion. - Caveats: Complex third-party orchestration applications may themselves own S3; their business governance is outside this source-boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit of operational reporting is connected by corrective feedback into normal current-control regulation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Inaccurate model outputs, weak artifacts or unmet criteria. A real Criteria LLM scorer exists for selected messages, but its returned Evaluation is not a shipped governance intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The optional model-backed scorer returns Pass, Score, Feedback to its caller; it does not independently interrogate ordinary multi-unit current control or own a returned corrective/withhold decision. - Decision owner: Optional scorer agent/model and adopter caller choosing scoring criteria/handling. No separately authorized S3 organizational corrective actor in the normal running app. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed evaluation JSON, model Run, optional diagnostics/test code, middleware confirmation and tool errors. - Closure path: Caller invokes evaluator with message → evaluator model outputs score/feedback → returned to caller; absent independently obtained conflicting operating evidence → S3 challenge → accountable correction/commitment revision returned into S1/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LLM judging is genuine model feedback, but optional endpoint called by an adopter is not by itself complementary audit with independent access and enforced correction. Model judge names or scoring output are insufficient. - Evidence: evaluator/criteria.go; evaluator/criteria.go; evaluator/criteria.go; evaluator/evaluator.go; flow/loop.go; middleware/confirm.go. - Basis: Source-backed evaluator input/output and separate workflow caller boundary, bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium; evaluator possibility explicitly inspected rather than dismissed. - Caveats: Specific external applications may wire evaluation to live corrective loops; outside declared first-party installed mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective environmental-intelligence and organizational capability-adaptation decision with accepted return into operating configuration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing longer-horizon integration/model/tool needs and shifting user tasks; session memory and skills may preserve context, but not select strategic organizational changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer configures model, tools, skills, recipe, memory and graph. Runtime can select operations, but no shipped autonomous future capability evaluation/approval/install right is established. - Decision owner: External developer/operator for configuration, task agent for current decisions, optional model evaluator for individual outputs; no operational S4 owner or legitimate parent-return future adaptation path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Skill loader, memory and history, model adapters, configurable ToolResolver, graph checkpoint/replay and Recipe loader. - Closure path: Current request may read memory/skill instructions and operate within them; absent future environment sensing → alternative capability selection → authorized changed agent/tool topology → subsequent operations reflecting an S4 decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Pluggable design, tool registration, resumed graph and long-term memory are infrastructure for extensibility, not evidence of prospective system-level intelligence and closure. - Evidence: agent.go; agent.go; agent.go; session.go; skills/loader.go; memory/memory.go; recipe/loader.go. - Basis: Bounded negative of supported dynamic extension/memory/graph mechanisms. - Confidence: Medium-high for standard first-party modes. - Caveats: A customized third-party self-improving agent could use these tools as S4, but none is first-party coupled here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party final legitimate values/purpose/organizational identity decision and returned binding policy control path at the application recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool authorization, model/config identity and task-level confirmations, not proven ultimate organizational mission conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer defines system instructions, model and tool set; optional ConfirmFunc allows/denies individual invocation; no function settles legitimate ultimate ethos/purpose for this app. - Decision owner: Developer/operator who authored application constraints and callback, plus deterministic middleware that enforces tool/invocation admission; none a packaged S5 purpose-governing organ. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: System instruction precedence, ToolResolver, middleware Confirm, stop/exit, session model and custom provider config. - Closure path: Current invocation → approval callback → allowed/interrupted execution; missing policy-purpose dispute → authorized final organizational judgment → returned revised governing purpose for present operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A named agent, static instructions or per-tool human confirmation is not automatically ultimate authority over the system&#39;s identity and legitimate direction. - Evidence: agent.go; agent.go; middleware/confirm.go; middleware/confirm.go; runner.go. - Basis: Explicit source-control and middleware scope, bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high for shipped first-party API/modes. - Caveats: An actual adopter organization may supply S5 externally; this is not a packaged Blades owner and no parent-governed return is evidenced.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/473cdf60f60b630a52de16f2450c621f550fc59b/assessments/blades.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 473cdf60f60b630a52de16f2450c621f550fc59b\">473cdf6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T00:15:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lvndry/jazz\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jazz</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-25\">2026-09-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lvndry/jazz/tree/957ad8ef522f9137f690c8acc319553f56e5bdb9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 957ad8ef522f9137f690c8acc319553f56e5bdb9\">957ad8e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform a user/workflow objective into tool-mediated environmental outcomes through repeated model reasoning, tool selection, execution and result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task requirements, tool/environment state, provider responses, command/file/web results, approval outcomes, context pressure, failures and user/queued input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive response/tool action and revise subsequent behavior from returned tool/environment evidence. - Decision owner: the active Jazz model agent in the primary or recursive child run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentRunner, agent loop, tool executor/registry, conversation/context manager, approvals, retry/budget guards, memory, persistence and presentation surfaces. - Closure path: objective/context → model completion chooses tool/action → tool runtime executes/observes environment → tool result is appended to the run → next model completion observes changed evidence and chooses subsequent action → task/environment state changes. - Boundary reachability: standard first-party path used by terminal, headless, workflow/chat and recursive sub-agent execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime machinery bounds and executes choices, but removing the model removes the substantive next-action judgment while leaving only transport/enforcement mechanisms. - Evidence: agent-loop.ts; agent-runner.ts; subagent-tools.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination relation is established at the assessed deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no complete qualifying inter-S1 interference/oscillation witness is established among Jazz operational agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S2 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic tool concurrency cap, recursive child depth/iteration bounds, background shell-job queue, workflow scheduler, peer messaging and approval serialization constrain execution but do not by themselves establish S2. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed standard path reconstructs distinct S1 agents → concrete inter-S1 disturbance → S2-specific attenuation → feedback changing subsequent S1 behavior. - Distinct S1 units: a parent can spawn one or more recursive child agents with their own fresh conversations and complete model/tool loops. - Inter-S1 disturbance: not established as a concrete organizational interaction. Generic contention for MAXCONCURRENTTOOLS is tool-runtime capacity and no child-specific disturbance is surfaced into child behavior; shell jobs are not autonomous S1s. - Attenuating coordination relation: generic tool scheduling, recursion depth bounds and job-worker concurrency prevent resource exhaustion but are not shown regulating a concrete child-to-child operational disturbance. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child final results return to the parent, but no coordination verdict about an inter-S1 disturbance is returned to affected child S1s or otherwise closes an S2 relation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it is deliberately not mapped as S2. spawnsubagent, askpeer, schedules and job fan-out are delegation/communication/execution primitives without the required inter-S1 disturbance-and-feedback witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no material S2 function is established, so ownership classification does not proceed. - Evidence: subagent-tools.ts; tool-executor.ts; job-queue-service.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system inside-and-now current-control loop is established at the assessed deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the parent agent decomposes its own task and the daemon/scheduler regulates execution mechanics, but no whole-deployment current operational variety is managed through a distinct S3 current view/decision loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established over current commitments/resources/priorities of all credited S1 units. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnsubagent, returned child results, workflow scheduler, job-batch status/cancel, approvals, daemon retries/recovery and normal run state. - Closure path: not applicable; delegation supplies a task and later a child result, but the standard path does not close whole-system current view → S3 judgment → current intervention across S1 commitments → renewed whole-system feedback. - Boundary reachability: the controls are first-party and reachable, but reachability/delegation does not establish the S3 function. - Whole-system current view: not established. spawnsubagent is a synchronous tool-style child run with a final result; no standard live child-tree list/status surface was established for the parent. - Current-control decision scope: the model can decide whether to spawn another bounded child, but no first-party path was found for persistent whole-tree steering, reassignment or interruption based on a current aggregate view. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no qualifying S3 function is established. Generic model orchestration of delegated subtasks remains part of current S1 task execution rather than a separately evidenced metasystemic regulator. - Evidence: subagent-tools.ts; scheduler-service.ts; job-worker.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary-audit closure is established at the assessed deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Jazz exposes transcripts, run history, telemetry, eval/development checks and can run review tasks, but no materially independent audit loop is established over ordinary runtime claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: logs/run history/telemetry, repository evals, GitHub PR-review workflow capability, generic recursive sub-agents and approval gates. - Closure path: not applicable; reviewed surfaces can observe or evaluate work but no standard runtime path closes complementary access → independent audit judgment → finding returned into current organizational control. - Claim being audited: no function-specific first-party claim/loop established for audit of the assessed Jazz organization. - Ordinary reporting path: normal model/tool transcript, tool results, metrics and workflow/job status. - Complementary access path: evals/review tasks can inspect outputs or repository state, but they are task/development surfaces rather than a distinct production audit channel over Jazz operations. - Independence boundary: no audit-specific actor/sensor boundary separate from ordinary runtime reporting was established. - Who acts on findings: a user or parent agent can act on a review result, but that generic composition is not a shipped S3 closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no S3 function is established before ownership classification. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/agent/tools/subagent-tools.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then organizational adaptation loop is established at the assessed deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: web/search/peer information and memory can influence current/future task responses, but no organizationally scoped external/prospective adaptation function is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S4 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web/search tools, askpeer, scheduled workflows, durable memory/context extraction, plugins/MCP and model/provider configuration. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed path reconstructs external/future-relevant sensing → explicit adaptation option development → adaptation judgment → return into present Jazz organizational capability/control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Jazz agents can autonomously research and remember information, but generic research/learning is part of task execution unless it participates in a demonstrated external-and-prospective organizational adaptation conversation. - Evidence: peer-tools.ts; packages/core/src/agent/context/memory-extractor.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the assessed deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: persona, system prompt, permissions, approval policy, tool grants and user rules constrain operation but are not shown as identity-level policy issues resolved by an ultimate authority loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S5 within the standard Jazz deployment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent JSON configuration, persona/system instructions, permissions/approvals, auto-approval policy, peer grants, tool allowlists and operator configuration. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed first-party path shows an identity/ultimate-policy issue reaching legitimate ultimate authority and returning as authoritative policy governing subsequent Jazz operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents operate within configured identity/permission boundaries; they are not shown exercising ultimate authority over those boundaries. Operator approval/configuration alone is not reclassified as S5. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/agent/tools/subagent-tools.ts; packages/core/src/agent/tools/peer-tools.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review - Confidence: high - Caveats: Jazz intentionally makes agent rules and permissions configurable; Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat configuration existence or ordinary approval as S5 without identity/ultimate-policy closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4a9a3df427159997173949c13597f68b727f2309/assessments/jazz.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4a9a3df427159997173949c13597f68b727f2309\">4a9a3df &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:18:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/THUDM/AgentRL\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentRL</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/THUDM/AgentRL/tree/6a73409d31ba695d383b978a8ad3ef400d90c054\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6a73409d31ba695d383b978a8ad3ef400d90c054\">6a73409 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: each rollout agent can execute a multiturn environment task autonomously and produce operational trajectories.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: Ray placement, task queues and worker pools coordinate training infrastructure, not interference among autonomous operational S1 units pursuing shared live commitments. The shallow S2=C interpretation is removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: Task Manager/trainer scheduling governs the training pipeline rather than current operational commitments of a viable multi-S1 system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: reward/loss computation evaluates training trajectories for optimization, not an independent complementary audit channel governing live S1 work.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: RL converts historical environment outcomes into updated model parameters, but it is an offline/development training loop rather than a distinct runtime S4 function scanning future external conditions and adapting organizational capability. The shallow S4=C interpretation is removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: reward design, tasks, training configuration and deployment purpose remain externally supplied.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c6f4922f94d64503565f75530e237dac6540e155/assessments/agentrl.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c6f4922f94d64503565f75530e237dac6540e155\">c6f4922 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T21:31:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Kocoro-lab/Shannon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shannon</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Kocoro-lab/Shannon/tree/391b619130281502d227a53dd97238c9791bf174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 391b619130281502d227a53dd97238c9791bf174\">391b619 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. AgentLoops perform bounded model/tool work and adapt subsequent actions from observations. The agent actor owns the next operational action within configured limits.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: —. Swarm Lead decomposition, delegation, result collection and convergence do not establish a separate first-party mechanism regulating a concrete inter-S1 interference/conflict/oscillation. The historical A promoted orchestration into S2 without the required disturbance/regulation witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: C. Budget management, preflight checks and backpressure regulate shared current execution capacity across the running system. The runtime supplies and enforces this whole-system resource-control path; applications configure its bounds.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. No materially different access path to operational reality was established. Validation, sandbox and policy checks remain controls on ordinary execution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. No first-party outside-and-then loop converts external/future environmental information into organizational adaptation and returns it to current capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Durable human approval gates ordinary risky/current actions. It does not establish a function-specific identity/ultimate-policy issue reaching legitimate S5 authority and returning as organizational policy. OPA/configuration surfaces likewise do not themselves close S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cfe27303e117e1fd3a79fc8f485f33caada591a4/assessments/shannon.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cfe27303e117e1fd3a79fc8f485f33caada591a4\">cfe2730 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-22T20:38:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/vinhnx/VTCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">VTCode</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-22\">2026-10-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/vinhnx/VTCode/tree/dc32271158bec3c37b81ad7bf57157a801307517\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dc32271158bec3c37b81ad7bf57157a801307517\">dc32271 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out user-scoped coding tasks through model-selected terminal/file/patch/search tools and iterative observation; optionally carry out bounded delegated subtasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Varying user requirements and source structures, build/test failures, provider responses, filesystem and terminal output, and findings during code changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The active model chooses task-specific tools/actions and subsequent continuation from returned tool outputs. First-party child agents have their own model-guided runner and tools within configured permissions. - Decision owner: VTCode AgentRunner model response and active child model sessions; typed tool registry executes selected actions under platform/user restrictions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model/provider adapter, tool dispatcher, sandbox and command policy, task tracker, session memory, auto-compaction, verification/continuation controls, child lifecycle statuses and bounded budgets. - Closure path: User task or delegated prompt → provider tool decision → executable tool action → result in session context → next model choice or completion/verification; child findings may return to parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reasoning over current source/output and choosing concrete actions is task-owned autonomy, even though permission policy, operator plan approval and provider contracts remain external constraints. - Boundary reachability: vtcode interactive and vtcode exec load this agent/tool runtime, and the registered subagent facade can construct first-party child AgentRunner sessions with effective workspace and permissions. - Evidence: CLI/agent flow, main task model/tool feedback loop, batched tool dispatch, child model-runner configuration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for first-party operational wiring; no empirical model-backed successful coding task is claimed. - Caveats: Child capabilities vary by spec, model and tool policies; the default explorer is intentionally read-only.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Detect and attenuate real interference between independent S1 coding cells, then return a compensating coordination decision into ongoing units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential concurrent child edits to the same project files, competing branch changes, conflicting integration results and resource collisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The controller spawns/waits and may create optional isolated worktrees. The completion hook&#39;s heuristic diff/merge is not a demonstrated returned inter-S1 adjustment: built-in child specs default to no worktree, the branch/worktree name handed to reconciliation differs from creation, and outcomes are logged rather than propagated as child/parent coordination decisions. - Decision owner: Fixed SubagentController lifecycle/worktree utilities and parent task delegator; no source-backed separate owner closing actual cross-S1 conflict detection/attenuation/return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child concurrency/depth caps, optional WorktreeManager, git worktree locks, read-only explorer permission, status/wait/close, heuristic unsafe-added-line check and git merge failure handling. - Closure path: Parent delegate → children perform bounded tasks → statuses/results returned; optional child worktree → completion reconciliation attempt. A conflict-aware correction received by independently operating affected children is not evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parallel worktrees can prevent direct write collisions and git may reject merges, but source does not show a working regulated inter-unit feedback relationship rather than isolation, delegation and a post-hoc integration attempt. - Evidence: built-in worker/explorer isolation none, generated worktree name on spawn, after-child reconciliation with spec.name, reconciler hard-codes main branch and logs result, git verify and merge. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; concurrency/worktree mechanisms are real but integration effectiveness is untested and a naming mismatch is visible in source. - Caveats: A repaired or externally coordinated worktree installation could produce new evidence for S2. This is not an assertion of any reproduced runtime merge failure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Make binding current whole-system resource, priority and commitment decisions over distinct viable S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple subagent requests, shared task progress, accumulated spend/time, incomplete subtasks and accepted/rejected local work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The parent can delegate, wait, cancel and integrate findings, while plan/build/evaluate controls one current task. No distinct whole-current portfolio tradeoff owner reassigns resources/commitments across operating cells on their shared current state. - Decision owner: Parent task agent for delegation and fixed controller for caps; no additional evidenced S3-specific manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Subagent status/notifications, max depth and concurrency, task tracker, runtime budget, session handoff and single-threaded spec/contract/evaluator revisions. - Closure path: Subtask prompt → child status/report → parent coding turn; plan spec → generator → task evaluator → possible retry. This is work delivery/QA inside S1, not governance of all current interdependent operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parent retains local task control, but performing decomposition does not prove whole-current current-control authority with accountable cross-unit commitments. - Evidence: subagent queue and concurrency, wait/result flow, single-threaded PBE boundary, task-local planner. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded assessment. - Caveats: Enterprise scheduling external to one session is a different recursion, not silently included here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Complement current management claims with sufficiently independent operational evidence and return actionable correction to S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect patches, false task completion, missed acceptance criteria, unsafe diffs and overconfident agent output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: verifyproposedchange can spawn a read-only verifier, and opt-in PBE uses a skeptical evaluator or a multi-model skeptic panel; they judge the local change/spec/verification, not the whole-current S3 operational account. Worktree reconciliation instead calls a deterministic line heuristic. - Decision owner: Task-level reviewer model/evaluator and heuristic diff gate; no S3 management-audit owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only explorer/verifier, explicit PASS/REJECT parsing, evaluator scorecard and high-severity findings, task tracker contracts, model skeptic panel, git changed-file summaries. - Closure path: Code changes and task spec → reviewer/evaluator findings → local S1 revision or user report. This is artifact/task QA; an S3 state or claim is not independently observed and corrected. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Separate models may challenge local work genuinely, but independence of a task reviewer is not the independence/authority of a complementary managerial audit channel. - Evidence: verifier agent and fail-closed verdict, worktree heuristic instead of model reviewer, evaluator and skeptic option, PBE single-threaded architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A QA tool can materially improve reliability without crossing the VSM S3 boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Model external future changes/opportunities and choose organizational capability adaptations with return to current management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing developer needs, model/tool ecosystem, future projects and software/repository practices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Plan/build/evaluate can ask a model to replan the current rejected implementation and record bounded, task-scoped generalization notes. Skills, context and scheduling may alter tasks, but no installed separate prospective environmental intelligence and S3-mediated capability choice is evidenced. - Decision owner: Local task planner/replanner or human config/skill author; no demonstrated S4 strategist. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task spec/contract, acceptance criteria, scorecard, task generalization notes with falsifiers, local session memory, external MCP/skills and opt-in durable vtcode schedule invocations. - Closure path: Current implementation → skeptical evaluation → revised task plan → continued main execution; no future external environment → alternative capability strategy → S3 handoff and adoption closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Genuine model re-planning is confined to present-task correctness; available external tools and task-triggered schedules do not prove autonomous anticipation or organizational adaptation. - Evidence: task-local reevaluation and replan, evidence-bounded generalization and evaluator prompt, runtime architecture and scheduling, loop engineering workflow scope. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within shipping first-party paths. - Caveats: Users may explicitly ask VTCode to research or implement future tooling; the general S1 capability to accept such prompts is not an autonomous first-party S4 mechanism.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimately settle ultimate organizational purpose, identity and governing values, including tensions between current operation and proposed future strategy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Untrusted workspace instructions, malicious commands, protected files, conflicting tool privileges and unsafe/unapproved implementation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator/user decides task and approves plans/workspaces; fixed tool policy and sandbox control allowable actions. The runtime has no legitimate ultimate identity/policy decision right beyond applying these choices. - Decision owner: User and application-defined command/workspace safety gates; not a first-party autonomous S5 holder. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planning review gate, workspace trust, ToolPolicyGateway, command policy, sandbox, AGENTS.md context, permission profiles, tool denial paths and human consent. - Closure path: User task/policy → tool catalog and dispatch approval → accepted/denied S1 action; plan finalization explicitly awaits user approval before build. No new binding agent-level ultimate policy is decided and returned to an S3/S4 balance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Enforcing preconfigured policy and producing a proposed plan is operational accountability, not legitimate authority to define VTCode&#39;s ultimate purpose or override the user. - Evidence: planning human handoff and read-only enforcement, security/model and command policy, configurable approval/sandbox components, project instructions as non-security context. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high on human and fixed-rule gate boundary; medium-high on absent S5. - Caveats: Code safety, review and honest handoffs are useful but not proof of S5 self-governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d6f173cbcb069780266193e1b2597ee7fef11a9d/assessments/vt/vtcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d6f173cbcb069780266193e1b2597ee7fef11a9d\">d6f173c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:41:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TencentCloudADP/youtu-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Youtu-Agent</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TencentCloudADP/youtu-agent/tree/c2caa539f4c95ae1c39ed24dc8a99cb3651e1d5d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c2caa539f4c95ae1c39ed24dc8a99cb3651e1d5d\">c2caa53 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: simple and worker agents autonomously choose tools/actions for bounded tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the planner statically decomposes and sequences worker tasks; workers do not mutually regulate shared constraints or interference. Generated workflow primitives likewise require application-defined coordination. The shallow S2=C interpretation is removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the planner owns a current task plan and worker selection, but lacks the persistent whole-system progress/resource/commitment regulation, feedback-based reassignment or replanning required for S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: no separate independent complementary audit channel over operational work was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: agent generation and training/practice can improve later behavior, but they are development/training mechanisms rather than a runtime prospective environmental-intelligence function governing adaptation. The shallow S4=C interpretation is removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: generated prompts/configuration, worker roster and overall mission/policy remain externally framed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d5872972b06cc594960efefa39b785e6b88271a/assessments/youtu-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d5872972b06cc594960efefa39b785e6b88271a\">2d58729 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:41:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QuantaAlpha/RepoMaster\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RepoMaster</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QuantaAlpha/RepoMaster/tree/9cf59930102970d61d6eb2f702fd936d3cda7f11\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9cf59930102970d61d6eb2f702fd936d3cda7f11\">9cf5993 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: repository/code agents autonomously explore, execute and react to task results inside their configured mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: scheduler→tool/agent routing and sequential fallback are delegation/orchestration. No mechanism was established for regulating interference, oscillation or shared constraints among multiple autonomous S1 units. The shallow S2=C interpretation is removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the scheduler manages a current problem-solving plan but does not expose whole-system authority over persistent shared resources, capacities or multi-S1 commitments.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: result evaluation is performed by the same scheduler trajectory; no separate complementary audit channel was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: web/repository search gathers external information for the current objective, not a prospective intelligence function responsible for adapting future system strategy/capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: modes, prompts, tools and termination policy are developer-defined.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d5872972b06cc594960efefa39b785e6b88271a/assessments/repomaster.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d5872972b06cc594960efefa39b785e6b88271a\">2d58729 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:41:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pi</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/tree/71dca871bc80b6bc97be37f0ca3189399d651fff\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 71dca871bc80b6bc97be37f0ca3189399d651fff\">71dca87 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: autonomous model/tool iteration is the core first-party abstraction.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: message queues and steering coordinate turns within one agent session; no mechanism was found for regulating interference among multiple autonomous S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: runtime callbacks and session controls do not create whole-system current authority over a multi-S1 organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: no independent/complementary audit path distinct from normal tool-loop execution was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: context preparation, compaction and model changes between turns support current execution rather than a distinct prospective environment-intelligence function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: system prompts, tools, providers and stop/steering policy are parent/application supplied.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d5872972b06cc594960efefa39b785e6b88271a/assessments/pi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d5872972b06cc594960efefa39b785e6b88271a\">2d58729 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T04:01:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MiroMindAI/MiroThinker\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MiroThinker</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MiroMindAI/MiroThinker/tree/1c4253f6774bf40314271a827304b842100e054c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1c4253f6774bf40314271a827304b842100e054c\">1c4253f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: research agents autonomously choose search/tool actions and iterate from evidence/results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: subagents are invoked as operational capabilities and return results to the main agent. Duplicate-query and rollback protections operate inside individual agent loops, not between autonomous S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: orchestration of current turns/subtasks does not establish whole-system regulation of shared resources, capacities or commitments.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: rollback/format checking is ordinary execution control, not a distinct complementary audit channel.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: broad external research is performed to answer the current task; post-training/trajectory refinement is not a runtime prospective intelligence function governing future organizational adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: models, prompts, tools and organizational topology remain externally configured.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c62c112b9468bc60e2a63b92623b0b69fd6248a6/assessments/mirothinker.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c62c112b9468bc60e2a63b92623b0b69fd6248a6\">c62c112 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T18:58:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TurixAI/TuriX-CUA\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TuriX-CUA</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TurixAI/TuriX-CUA/tree/88c777bddfec9796667445873886a3563aa27246\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 88c777bddfec9796667445873886a3563aa27246\">88c777b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: First-party model-directed GUI task execution with observation/action/result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing macOS screens, UI element locations, login prompts, incomplete subtasks, unexpected app responses and errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Brain LLM interprets screenshots and chooses a current procedural goal; Actor LLM chooses corresponding GUI actions and arguments; first-party agent loop decides when to continue/stop on current outcome. - Decision owner: One first-party Agent system implementing model-driven Brain/Actor decision turns; inference providers supply models, not an additional prebuilt external computer-use harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MacUITreeBuilder screenshot/accessibility capture, Controller action registry, structured output parser, task history, action results, failure/step limits, optional Planner/skills/search/memory. - Closure path: GUI screenshot/previous action → Brain nextgoal and analysis → Actor action JSON → installed Controller.multiact executes on macOS → next screenshot/action history → Brain reevaluates and selects next goal. - Boundary reachability: examples/main.py instantiates shipped Agent with supplied Brain/Actor/Memory and optional Planner models, and calls Agent.run(maxsteps); run loops brainstep and actorstep, which invokes first-party Controller.multiact. No separate tool-providing external agent is required for this code path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Models make concrete runtime choice of next goal and GUI actions; execution is not limited to static hard-coded macros. The orchestrator/Controller still enforce data/step/action bounds. - Evidence: examples/main.py; src/agent/service.py; src/agent/views.py; src/controller/service.py; src/mac/actions.py. - Basis: Source-level reachable model GUI observation/feedback loop. - Confidence: High for source reachability; no external macOS/live-model execution attempted. - Caveats: Requires macOS native frameworks and a working configured provider; real task performance not established.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party mutual attenuation of interference among distinct independently operating S1 units proven. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A model Brain/Actor disagreement concerns one task&#39;s sequence and action quality, not a documented conflict among separate concurrent S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No coordinator detecting and attenuating actual contention across separate worker tasks, desktop agents or resource reservations. - Decision owner: One Agent&#39;s sequential execution scheduler, which passes single-task state between subroles. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Structured role outputs, action list, one-step loop, last-step history, optional plan and memory. - Closure path: Result flows between Brain and Actor for the same task; no separate inter-S1 disturbance → coordination choice → changed sibling activity feedback path evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Brain, Actor, Memory and Planner are model calls in a single operation. Distinct LLM configurations do not create distinct organizational S1 operations. - Evidence: src/agent/service.py; src/agent/plannerservice.py; src/agent/messagemanager/service.py; examples/main.py. - Basis: Bounded negative structural search at frozen default distribution. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An external multi-agent system may run multiple instances but its coordination is not supplied by this pinned core.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately owned current whole-system commitments/resources regulation beyond the one task&#39;s own planner/controller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current GUI subgoal failure, incomplete plan steps, retries and model output errors within one operational task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Brain chooses current next goal and optional Planner composes current user&#39;s task; no actor chooses among organization-wide competing active tasks, priorities, resources or responsibilities. - Decision owner: One task-level Brain/Actor agent; user supplies task, and deterministic loop enforces max steps/failure cap. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Current screenshot evaluation, Planner plan generation, pause/stop controls, max failures/steps. - Closure path: Next goal → individual task action → next task observation, not separate whole-operational control → reallocation/return across concurrent operational units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Brain is a task planner/evaluator, not whole-system S3 despite &#39;planner&#39; labels, and the task&#39;s bounded loop is not a delegated operational-management position. - Evidence: src/agent/service.py; src/agent/plannerservice.py; examples/config.json. - Basis: Bounded negative review of decisive assignment and resource authority. - Confidence: Medium-high within declared single-run operational recursion. - Caveats: Additional multi-agent orchestration in an external OpenClaw runtime is not credited to TuriX-CUA.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary access/challenge of operational claims plus corrective return separate from ordinary execution was evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Action success/failure and changed UI state are ordinary primary task feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The same Brain that determines next task decisions also judges previous-step screenshot success; Controller returns local action results, not an independently owned auditor verdict. - Decision owner: Within-task Brain/model and deterministic controller/result parser. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Screenshot comparisons, stepevaluate, saved action histories, action errors and in-task memory summaries. - Closure path: Actor executes → Brain sees next screenshot and self-checks → Brain picks task continuation. This is first-party S1 feedback, not an alternative independent auditor collecting separately scoped operational evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Prompt-separated Brain versus Actor gives functional division but same run/controller authority. A different model role alone is not enough to demonstrate independent audit rights. - Evidence: src/agent/service.py; src/agent/prompts.py; src/agent/views.py; src/controller/service.py. - Basis: Bounded negative ownership/independence comparison. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A user can manually inspect a GUI, but no shipped agent-independent audit return is evidenced.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No supported external prospective change-of-organizational-capability option and return loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unseen webpage changes and task uncertainty are current operational disturbances; model/GUI capabilities are specified through configured endpoints/skills. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Optional Planner may search or choose a skill for the present user task; Memory compresses/retrieves prior action context. Neither chooses/testing a new future capability for the harness and returns it to subsequent operation. - Decision owner: Task-driven model roles and operator-managed code/skill/model settings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PlannerSearch and skill selection, Memory LLM summaries, record persistence and resumed action history. - Closure path: Search/record → better present task steps; no current-capability evidence → prospective alternatives → adaptation selection/application → later organizational operation closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Learning a webpage or summarizing an execution trace is not future adaptation of the computer-use organization&#39;s capability. - Evidence: src/agent/plannerservice.py; src/agent/service.py; src/utils/skills.py; src/utils/recordstore.py. - Basis: Bounded review of optional planning/search/memory and configured skills. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The operator could install new skills manually outside the runtime; not evidence of an installed S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity/policy decision rights or returned resolution for the computer-use system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Human login/preferences, step-stop limits, model instructions and selected provider/tool capabilities are task-level constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator chooses starting task and may cancel; Brain askhuman asks for clarification. No supported actor decides system-wide ultimate ethos/identity conflict and returns binding policy. - Decision owner: Human task originator and externally maintained software settings; no function-specific S5 owner evidenced in the shipped harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: User task prompt, askhuman, pause/stop and force-stop hotkey, configured model API and skills. - Closure path: User clarifies an input or cancels a task; there is no independent policy controversy → ultimate authority decision → policy return to organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static prompts/user approval do not equal agent-owned or parent-governed S5 under the active Methodology. - Evidence: src/agent/views.py; src/agent/service.py; examples/main.py; examples/config.json. - Basis: Bounded negative source review of human and task authority. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A deploying organization may have its own user approval policy, outside the credited system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/093016a38ca74a0929f3e6eb9d6ac5a7a62fa831/assessments/turix-cua.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 093016a38ca74a0929f3e6eb9d6ac5a7a62fa831\">093016a &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
